141
00:07:57,392 --> 00:07:59,930
with Mars Rover and others.

142
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:00,450
All right.

143
00:08:00,450 --> 00:08:02,780
So these are we're going to
assess a bunch of things.

144
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:04,280
And we're going to
start by thinking

145
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:06,107
about staying in
the tabular land,

146
00:08:06,107 --> 00:08:07,940
so staying where we can
write down the value

147
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:10,340
function as a vector,
and then trying

148
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:14,170
to learn how to make optimal
decisions in that case.

149
00:08:14,170 --> 00:08:17,410
So let's first just talk about
the idea of generalized policy

150
00:08:17,410 --> 00:08:18,340
improvement.

151
00:08:18,340 --> 00:08:21,310
So we've seen before
this idea of alternating

152
00:08:21,310 --> 00:08:24,185
between policy evaluation
and policy improvement.

153
00:08:24,185 --> 00:08:26,560
And now we're going to think
about that for slightly more

154
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:27,713
general cases of policies.

155
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:33,760
So what we just said here
is that if the policy is

156
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:35,890
deterministic, we can't
compute the state action

157
00:08:35,890 --> 00:08:38,620
value for any action
that's not the policy.

158
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:41,799
And so what we'd like
to be able to do now

159
00:08:41,799 --> 00:08:44,059
is to have kind
of more coverage.

160
00:08:44,059 --> 00:08:46,660
And to do that, we're going
to have stochastic policies.

161
00:08:46,660 --> 00:08:48,680
Because if the
policy is stochastic,

162
00:08:48,680 --> 00:08:51,050
then we'll try multiple
actions in the same state.

163
00:08:51,050 --> 00:08:54,610
And we can use that data
to estimate the Q-function.

164
00:08:54,610 --> 00:08:57,190
So we're staying in what we're
calling model-free policy

165
00:08:57,190 --> 00:08:59,560
iteration, meaning we're not
trying to explicitly build

166
00:08:59,560 --> 00:09:01,300
a dynamics or reward model.

167
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:04,130
We're just trying to directly
estimate a Q-function.

168
00:09:04,130 --> 00:09:05,920
And once we have a
Q-function, then we

169
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:10,230
can extract from it an argmax
policy or something else.

170
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:11,440
Yeah.

171
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:17,920
And we're now going to be using
an estimated Q, because we

172
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:27,440
will be estimating Q from
data directly from experience.

173
00:09:29,900 --> 00:09:30,400
All right.

174
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:32,567
So this is going to introduce
this general challenge

175
00:09:32,567 --> 00:09:36,000
of exploration, which is we can
only learn about the things we

176
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:36,960
try in the world.

177
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:39,850
So this is just like the-- can't
know how much better or worse

178
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:42,720
your life would be right now if
you're drinking coffee at Coupa.

179
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:43,540
Same thing.

180
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:46,500
Like we can only learn about
the actions that we take.

181
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:50,530
And so we need to learn
about actions by trying them.

182
00:09:50,530 --> 00:09:52,470
So we need to explore.

183
00:09:52,470 --> 00:09:55,390
But the downside in general
is if we try new actions,

184
00:09:55,390 --> 00:09:57,870
we are spending less
time using our knowledge

185
00:09:57,870 --> 00:10:00,390
to make good decisions.

186
00:10:00,390 --> 00:10:04,240
So you might imagine that
you can act randomly always.

187
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:06,870
And that would work for like
learning a lot about the world

188
00:10:06,870 --> 00:10:08,920
and learning a lot
about Q-functions.

189
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:10,718
But you wouldn't be finding--

190
00:10:10,718 --> 00:10:12,510
you wouldn't be acting
using that knowledge

191
00:10:12,510 --> 00:10:14,370
to try to gain high reward.

192
00:10:14,370 --> 00:10:17,430
So this is known as the general
challenge between exploration

193
00:10:17,430 --> 00:10:20,250
and exploitation.

194
00:10:20,250 --> 00:10:22,590
How much time do we spend
exploring and getting

195
00:10:22,590 --> 00:10:24,780
new data about things
that might be good?

196
00:10:24,780 --> 00:10:28,320
Versus how much of the time do
we exploit our knowledge of how

197
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:31,030
the world works, according
to the data we have so far,

198
00:10:31,030 --> 00:10:33,190
to try to make good decisions?

199
00:10:33,190 --> 00:10:34,850
And this will come up a lot.

200
00:10:34,850 --> 00:10:38,920
There's really deep questions
around here about thinking of,

201
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:41,480
how do we quantify our
uncertainty in our knowledge?

202
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:43,450
And then how do we
propagate that uncertainty

203
00:10:43,450 --> 00:10:45,190
into the value of
that uncertainty

204
00:10:45,190 --> 00:10:46,838
for downstream decision making?

205
00:10:46,838 --> 00:10:49,130
So we'll see a lot more about
that later in the course.

206
00:10:49,130 --> 00:10:52,340
And this continues to be a
really active area of research.

207
00:10:52,340 --> 00:10:53,752
This is not at all solved.

208
00:10:53,752 --> 00:10:55,210
But here, we're
just going to start

209
00:10:55,210 --> 00:10:57,070
to see some simple
methods to try

210
00:10:57,070 --> 00:11:00,640
to tackle this challenge of
balancing between these two

211
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:02,700
things.

212
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:05,310
So one of the simplest things
you could imagine doing

213
00:11:05,310 --> 00:11:08,130
is what's called epsilon greedy.

214
00:11:08,130 --> 00:11:09,588
And the idea with
epsilon greedy is

215
00:11:09,588 --> 00:11:11,922
you're going to just spend
some of the time doing things

216
00:11:11,922 --> 00:11:14,250
randomly and some of the
times, doing things the best

217
00:11:14,250 --> 00:11:16,860
way you know how,
because you're kind

218
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:18,420
of exploiting that knowledge.

219
00:11:18,420 --> 00:11:20,160
So if we just have
a finite number

220
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:22,982
of actions, because right now,
we're still in the tabular case.

221
00:11:22,982 --> 00:11:24,690
So we just have a
finite number of states

222
00:11:24,690 --> 00:11:26,170
and a finite number of actions.

223
00:11:26,170 --> 00:11:31,640
Then epsilon greedy policy
says, with high probability--

224
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:33,532
so we have some epsilon here.

225
00:11:33,532 --> 00:11:34,990
Epsilon is going
to be less than 1.

226
00:11:34,990 --> 00:11:37,680
It could be like probability--
it could be 0.1, for example.

227
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:39,270
So with high
probability, you're going

228
00:11:39,270 --> 00:11:42,480
to take whatever
action maximizes your Q

229
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:44,653
value in your current state.

230
00:11:44,653 --> 00:11:46,070
So you're going
to kind of exploit

231
00:11:46,070 --> 00:11:48,620
your knowledge for whatever
your state action value says.

232
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:51,980
And you're going to do that with
probability 1 minus epsilon.

233
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:55,740
And then otherwise, you're going
to take an action at random.

234
00:11:55,740 --> 00:11:58,840
And so when you pick an
action uniformly at random,

235
00:11:58,840 --> 00:12:00,970
it might be one of-- the
same one as the argmax,

236
00:12:00,970 --> 00:12:02,490
or it might be a different one.

237
00:12:02,490 --> 00:12:04,890
But either way, the main
idea is that essentially, you

238
00:12:04,890 --> 00:12:08,130
spend 1 minus epsilon
percentage of the time

239
00:12:08,130 --> 00:12:11,130
being greedy with
respect to your knowledge

240
00:12:11,130 --> 00:12:13,205
and epsilon percent spend
time acting randomly.

241
00:12:16,710 --> 00:12:18,783
So it's like maybe
you say, OK, I'm

242
00:12:18,783 --> 00:12:20,950
committed to trying out new
things at my restaurant.

243
00:12:20,950 --> 00:12:22,740
So once a week, I will
try a random dish.

244
00:12:22,740 --> 00:12:25,410
And the other six days,
I'll pick whatever I like--

245
00:12:25,410 --> 00:12:29,510
like whatever I've liked in the
past, and it's always been good.

246
00:12:29,510 --> 00:12:31,140
So this is a pretty
simple strategy.

247
00:12:31,140 --> 00:12:34,160
This is not trying to have
a deep notion of uncertainty

248
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:35,730
or trying to quantify that.

249
00:12:35,730 --> 00:12:40,160
But nevertheless, this
can be pretty effective.

250
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:43,970
So in particular, we can prove
things about policy improvement

251
00:12:43,970 --> 00:12:45,950
with epsilon greedy policies.

252
00:12:45,950 --> 00:12:48,050
So what we proved in
the past is that if you

253
00:12:48,050 --> 00:12:51,500
do policy iteration, when you
know the dynamics and reward

254
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:54,780
models, you are guaranteed
to monotonically improve.

255
00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:56,940
So each round of
policy iteration,

256
00:12:56,940 --> 00:12:59,180
either you would stay the
same, in which case, you'd

257
00:12:59,180 --> 00:13:00,710
