00:23:42,320 --> 00:23:42,820
OK.

505
00:23:42,820 --> 00:23:44,450
And one thing just
to note here--

506
00:23:44,450 --> 00:23:46,520
and I think this
is a question 2.

507
00:23:46,520 --> 00:23:50,140
So just to be clear here, as
you're thinking about this,

508
00:23:50,140 --> 00:23:53,330
so this is like an approximation
of policy iteration.

509
00:23:53,330 --> 00:23:55,480
So we're kind of doing
policy evaluation and then

510
00:23:55,480 --> 00:23:57,490
policy improvement.

511
00:23:57,490 --> 00:24:01,360
But it's helpful to think about
kind of how much time we're

512
00:24:01,360 --> 00:24:03,340
spending doing policy
improvement versus policy

513
00:24:03,340 --> 00:24:04,460
evaluation.

514
00:24:04,460 --> 00:24:07,390
So what this is saying
here is that, you're

515
00:24:07,390 --> 00:24:10,480
going to sample one episode.

516
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:13,695
And then you're going
to do policy evaluation.

517
00:24:13,695 --> 00:24:14,195
OK.

518
00:24:14,195 --> 00:24:16,820
This is all just one episode.

519
00:24:16,820 --> 00:24:18,980
So it's like I'm
going to play one--

520
00:24:18,980 --> 00:24:23,830
I'm going to play until I
win or lose at Breakout once

521
00:24:23,830 --> 00:24:25,960
under a particular policy.

522
00:24:25,960 --> 00:24:28,100
And then I'm going
to change my policy.

523
00:24:28,100 --> 00:24:31,540
And then I'm going to play
with my new policy once.

524
00:24:31,540 --> 00:24:35,050
And then I'm going
to change my policy.

525
00:24:35,050 --> 00:24:38,050
And some of those games might
be really long, or some of them

526
00:24:38,050 --> 00:24:39,590
might be really short.

527
00:24:39,590 --> 00:24:40,090
Yeah.

528
00:24:40,090 --> 00:24:42,800
So this is-- just
to clarify, this

529
00:24:42,800 --> 00:24:44,990
is like representing
what you do after playing

530
00:24:44,990 --> 00:24:46,640
one game in a breakout?

531
00:24:46,640 --> 00:24:52,920
So then I think I might just
be confused, like, [INAUDIBLE].

532
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:55,790
When they're just like--
if I'm playing a game,

533
00:24:55,790 --> 00:24:58,110
the episodes just follow
one after the other.

534
00:24:58,110 --> 00:25:01,520
So isn't there just--
there's just one kth episode?

535
00:25:01,520 --> 00:25:03,120
There's one kth episode.

536
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:06,727
Yeah so like k is like
I play-- so if k is 1,

537
00:25:06,727 --> 00:25:08,060
I'm going to play my first game.

538
00:25:08,060 --> 00:25:09,893
And I'm going to play
it until I win or lose

539
00:25:09,893 --> 00:25:10,980
or until the game ends.

540
00:25:10,980 --> 00:25:12,170
So maybe Breakout finishes.

541
00:25:12,170 --> 00:25:15,650
Or maybe I'm playing Tetris,
and like I fail, and I die.

542
00:25:15,650 --> 00:25:17,180
And that is one episode.

543
00:25:17,180 --> 00:25:19,640
And I'm going to use that to
then update my Q-function.

544
00:25:19,640 --> 00:25:21,800
Then I'm going to change it and
say, OK, well, my next round,

545
00:25:21,800 --> 00:25:23,050
I'm going to play differently.

546
00:25:23,050 --> 00:25:26,040
And then I play Tetris
again until I fail.

547
00:25:26,040 --> 00:25:28,860
And then I see what
the total points are.

548
00:25:28,860 --> 00:25:31,020
I update my Q-function,
and I repeat.

549
00:25:31,020 --> 00:25:33,062
And some of those episodes
might be really short.

550
00:25:33,062 --> 00:25:34,645
So maybe the first
time-- particularly

551
00:25:34,645 --> 00:25:36,780
for these agents, the first
time they play Tetris,

552
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:39,890
maybe they lose in like 10
steps, might be a really short

553
00:25:39,890 --> 00:25:41,330
10 step.

554
00:25:41,330 --> 00:25:43,030
Later, maybe they
play for a long time.

555
00:25:43,030 --> 00:25:44,780
But in general, I've
not told you anything

556
00:25:44,780 --> 00:25:46,470
about how long
these episodes are.

557
00:25:46,470 --> 00:25:48,137
They might be really short.

558
00:25:48,137 --> 00:25:49,345
Or they might be really long.

559
00:25:52,620 --> 00:25:54,612
OK.

560
00:25:54,612 --> 00:25:56,545
And I think one
useful way I find

561
00:25:56,545 --> 00:25:58,170
to think about this
is that think about

562
00:25:58,170 --> 00:26:00,565
if they're really short,
like really, really short.

563
00:26:00,565 --> 00:26:02,190
Like I take two steps,
and I just fail.

564
00:26:02,190 --> 00:26:03,440
I did something really dumb.

565
00:26:03,440 --> 00:26:08,010
So in that case, think
about whether your Q would

566
00:26:08,010 --> 00:26:11,750
be a good estimate of Q pi k.

567
00:26:11,750 --> 00:26:14,000
Like, would it be good if
you've only seen two states?

568
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:15,083
Or would it be pretty bad?

569
00:26:20,950 --> 00:26:22,700
So why don't you turn
to someone near you?

570
00:26:22,700 --> 00:26:24,320
I think most people
have voted if you have

571
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:25,260
written something you have.

572
00:26:25,260 --> 00:26:27,218
But why don't you check
and see what you think.

573
00:28:03,672 --> 00:28:06,450
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

574
00:30:09,770 --> 00:30:10,270
OK.

575
00:30:10,270 --> 00:30:10,640
Awesome.

576
00:30:10,640 --> 00:30:12,220
I'm hearing a lot of
really good discussion.

577
00:30:12,220 --> 00:30:14,137
But I'm going to interrupt
you, because I want

578
00:30:14,137 --> 00:30:16,050
to make sure we get to DQN.

579
00:30:16,050 --> 00:30:17,580
So this is where--

580
00:30:17,580 --> 00:30:20,460
so one of the reasons that I
bring up this particular example

581
00:30:20,460 --> 00:30:22,205
is that here, it's tabular.

582
00:30:22,205 --> 00:30:23,830
I mean, these are a
little bit smaller,

583
00:30:23,830 --> 00:30:25,030
so it's a bit easier to see.

584
00:30:25,030 --> 00:30:27,363
But essentially, what I kind
of want you guys to get out

585
00:30:27,363 --> 00:30:30,060
of today is that it
should be sort of shocking

586
00:30:30,060 --> 00:30:31,665
that reinforcement
learning works.

587
00:30:31,665 --> 00:30:32,340
[CHUCKLES]

588
00:30:32,340 --> 00:30:33,930
And we're not going
to have time to go

589
00:30:33,930 --> 00:30:35,972
through all the deep
mathematical reasons for why

590
00:30:35,972 --> 00:30:37,660
it does work sometimes
in this class.

591
00:30:37,660 --> 00:30:39,360
But I'm happy to
give people pointers.

592
00:30:39,360 --> 00:30:42,010
But so there's several things
that are really kind of odd,

593
00:30:42,010 --> 00:30:44,740
if you start to think about
this, when you go through this.

594
00:30:44,740 --> 00:30:48,100
So first of all, Q is not
an estimate of Q pi k.

595
00:30:48,100 --> 00:30:50,520
It is not, because
it is averaging

596
00:30:50,520 --> 00:30:54,300
over policies that are
changing every episode

597
00:30:54,300 --> 00:30:56,230
or potentially changing
every episode, right?

598
00:30:56,230 --> 00:30:59,430
Because in fact, in
general, it will be, right,

599
00:30:59,430 --> 00:31:01,620
because we're decaying epsilon.

600
00:31:01,620 --> 00:31:03,210
So we're changing
epsilon each round,

601
00:31:03,210 --> 00:31:05,710
which means we're making things
more and more deterministic.

602
00:31:05,710 --> 00:31:08,540
But in addition to that,
our Q might be changing.

603
00:31:08,540 --> 00:31:12,400
So essentially, I'm just
trying a policy one round.

604
00:31:12,400 --> 00:31:16,780
And then I update my Q, and
then I try something again.

605
00:31:16,780 --> 00:31:19,205
And sort of extreme
example of this

606
00:31:19,205 --> 00:31:21,580
would be like flipping a coin
once and deciding whether--

607
00:31:21,580 --> 00:31:23,630
what its bias is or
something like that.

608
00:31:23,630 --> 00:31:26,450
That's just not very much
data to do this evaluation.

609
00:31:26,450 --> 00:31:28,360
And also, you're
averaging this over

610
00:31:28,360 --> 00:31:30,580
many, many different policies.

611
00:31:30,580 --> 00:31:33,205
So Q is not an
estimate of Q pi k.

612
00:31:33,205 --> 00:31:36,550
It's this weird weighted average
of all the previous data and all

613
00:31:36,550 --> 00:31:39,810
the policies you've done before.

614
00:31:39,810 --> 00:31:42,995
Q should not be an estimate
of like the [? big ?] changes

615
00:31:42,995 --> 00:31:45,960
in the latest k, k minus 1?

616
00:31:45,960 --> 00:31:47,410
Well, but not really.

617
00:31:47,410 --> 00:31:50,070
Because I mean you've
averaged in that part.

618
00:31:50,070 --> 00:31:52,560
That part's from pi k
plus 1, but this old thing

619
00:31:52,560 --> 00:31:54,990
was over all of your-- is like
this weird weighted average

620
00:31:54,990 --> 00:31:57,030
of all the other
policies you've tried.

621
00:31:57,030 --> 00:31:59,850
So yes, it is that,
but also like that

622
00:31:59,850 --> 00:32:01,620
plus all the other policies.

623
00:32:01,620 --> 00:32:04,890
So it's this weird thing, right?

624
00:32:04,890 --> 00:32:06,750
The second thing is
that we're only doing--

625
00:32:06,750 --> 00:32:08,640
and I was talking to
some people about this.

626
00:32:08,640 --> 00:32:13,013
We're only doing one rollout
to try to evaluate a policy.

627
00:32:13,013 --> 00:32:15,180
And you might imagine there's
a lot of stochasticity

628
00:32:15,180 --> 00:32:17,373
like even in something
like some games,

629
00:32:17,373 --> 00:32:20,040
there's like random rolls of the
dice and stuff like that, which

630
00:32:20,040 --> 00:32:21,520
means even with
the same strategy,

631
00:32:21,520 --> 00:32:23,970
you might get different
outcomes each time.

632
00:32:23,970 --> 00:32:27,090
So it'd be like if you drove
to SF, and you did it once.

633
00:32:27,090 --> 00:32:28,350
And there was no traffic.

634
00:32:28,350 --> 00:32:29,850
And so you're like,
I can always get

635
00:32:29,850 --> 00:32:33,160
to SF in like, I don't know,
20 minutes on the highway.

636
00:32:33,160 --> 00:32:34,870
But for those of you
that drive to SF,

637
00:32:34,870 --> 00:32:36,690
you would know that often,
there's lots of traffic.

638
00:32:36,690 --> 00:32:38,310
And so you would
need to average over

639
00:32:38,310 --> 00:32:42,460
many rounds of doing this to see
how good a particular route is.

640
00:32:42,460 --> 00:32:44,950
So the weird thing
here is that we're just

641
00:32:44,950 --> 00:32:47,150
doing kind of one rollout.

642
00:32:47,150 --> 00:32:49,793
We're averaging into this
weird Q thing, which is now

643
00:32:49,793 --> 00:32:51,460
going to be this
weighted average of all

644
00:32:51,460 --> 00:32:53,260
the policies we've done.

645
00:32:53,260 --> 00:32:56,140
And we have this
weird epsilon thing.

646
00:32:56,140 --> 00:32:59,620
And it should not be clear
yet that we will necessarily

647
00:32:59,620 --> 00:33:01,442
converge to Q star.

648
00:33:01,442 --> 00:33:03,400
Like we are getting more
and more deterministic

649
00:33:03,400 --> 00:33:06,370
over time, because
we're reducing epsilon.

650
00:33:06,370 --> 00:33:08,302
So we're reducing epsilon
here towards zero.

651
00:33:08,302 --> 00:33:10,510
Eventually, we're going to
converge towards something

652
00:33:10,510 --> 00:33:12,010
deterministic.

653
00:33:12,010 --> 00:33:13,840
But you may or may
not be convinced yet

654
00:33:13,840 --> 00:33:16,545
that the thing we're going to
converge to is actually Q star.

655
00:33:19,630 --> 00:33:23,650
So fortunately, there are
some sufficient conditions

656
00:33:23,650 --> 00:33:26,260
under which we can guarantee
that this sort of thing

657
00:33:26,260 --> 00:33:28,180
will converge to Q star.

658
00:33:28,180 --> 00:33:31,340
And really, it's quite
beautiful that this works.

659
00:33:31,340 --> 00:33:34,210
So one is what's called
greedy in the limit

660
00:33:34,210 --> 00:33:37,480
of infinite exploration or GLIE.

661
00:33:37,480 --> 00:33:41,200
So the idea in this case is that
if you can ensure that all state

662
00:33:41,200 --> 00:33:44,230
action pairs are visited an
infinite number of times,

663
00:33:44,230 --> 00:33:46,720
meaning the number
of counts that you

664
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:48,730
have for a particular
state and action pair

665
00:33:48,730 --> 00:33:53,440
goes to infinity for
all states and actions--

666
00:33:53,440 --> 00:33:54,390
this is for all.

667
00:33:59,080 --> 00:34:00,980
And the behavior policy.

668
00:34:00,980 --> 00:34:02,750
And what I mean by the
behavior policy is,

669
00:34:02,750 --> 00:34:04,417
this is the policy
you're actually using

670
00:34:04,417 --> 00:34:06,550
to make decisions in the world.

671
00:34:06,550 --> 00:34:07,780
And it will be important.

672
00:34:07,780 --> 00:34:10,113
There'll be distinctions
between this and other policies

673
00:34:10,113 --> 00:34:13,270
soon, which is why we
call this behavior policy.

674
00:34:13,270 --> 00:34:15,760
If the behavior-- so if
you sample state action

675
00:34:15,760 --> 00:34:19,300
pairs an infinite number of
times and your behavior policy

676
00:34:19,300 --> 00:34:23,110
converges to the greedy policy,
which means that asymptotically,

677
00:34:23,110 --> 00:34:25,600
the action you select
in a state is exactly

678
00:34:25,600 --> 00:34:30,159
equal to the argmax of your
Q-function with probability 1.

679
00:34:30,159 --> 00:34:32,320
So you're just getting more
and more deterministic.

680
00:34:32,320 --> 00:34:34,980
So then you were being
greedy in the limit

681
00:34:34,980 --> 00:34:37,260
of infinite
exploration that says

682
00:34:37,260 --> 00:34:41,139
that you're exploring everything
an infinite number of times.

683
00:34:41,139 --> 00:34:44,530
You're always continuing to
try all actions in all states.

684
00:34:44,530 --> 00:34:46,780
But you're getting more
and more deterministic.

685
00:34:46,780 --> 00:34:49,850
So this is what it
means to be GLIE.

686
00:34:49,850 --> 00:34:54,570
If you have a GLIE algorithm--

687
00:34:54,570 --> 00:34:56,639
and I'll just note
here, like a simple way

688
00:34:56,639 --> 00:35:01,020
to do this is to do e-greedy,
where epsilon is reduced

689
00:35:01,020 --> 00:35:03,170
to 0 at the following rate.

690
00:35:06,760 --> 00:35:08,440
Yeah, so we'd have this--

691
00:35:08,440 --> 00:35:10,930
so that's a simple one.

692
00:35:10,930 --> 00:35:16,665
And visit all states.

693
00:35:21,390 --> 00:35:23,070
And that should
hold-- as long as you

694
00:35:23,070 --> 00:35:24,990
have an e-greedy
strategy, then you

695
00:35:24,990 --> 00:35:27,372
will be able to visit
all states and actions.

696
00:35:27,372 --> 00:35:29,580
So you're going to be visiting
all states and actions

697
00:35:29,580 --> 00:35:32,730
under this GLIE strategy.

698
00:35:32,730 --> 00:35:35,100
Then under that, the
Monte Carlo algorithm

699
00:35:35,100 --> 00:35:37,860
I just showed you for
tabular representations

700
00:35:37,860 --> 00:35:42,150
will converge to Q
star, which means

701
00:35:42,150 --> 00:35:44,260
as long as you decay
epsilon at this rate,

702
00:35:44,260 --> 00:35:45,918
you are actually
converging to Q star.

703
00:35:45,918 --> 00:35:47,710
You're getting more
and more deterministic.

704
00:35:47,710 --> 00:35:49,543
You're still visiting
all states and actions

705
00:35:49,543 --> 00:35:50,740
an infinite number of times.

706
00:35:50,740 --> 00:35:53,820
And this procedure is guaranteed
to asymptotically get you

707
00:35:53,820 --> 00:35:56,230
to the optimal Q-function,
which is pretty cool.

708
00:35:56,230 --> 00:35:59,950
And it should be
somewhat surprising.

709
00:35:59,950 --> 00:36:00,450
All right.

710
00:36:00,450 --> 00:36:02,070
So that is GLIE.

711
00:36:02,070 --> 00:36:04,130
And that is of
the reasons why we

712
00:36:04,130 --> 00:36:06,510
like to think about
e-greedy algorithms,

713
00:36:06,510 --> 00:36:08,060
because they have
this nice property

714
00:36:08,060 --> 00:36:10,860
that we can prove that we are
going to get an optimal policy,

715
00:36:10,860 --> 00:36:13,235
even though all we're doing
is we're acting in the world.

716
00:36:13,235 --> 00:36:14,893
And we're getting this data.

717
00:36:14,893 --> 00:36:17,060
Now, what you should be
thinking about at this point

718
00:36:17,060 --> 00:36:17,730
is that, all right.

719
00:36:17,730 --> 00:36:19,680
Here's the Monte Carlo
approach to doing this.

720
00:36:19,680 --> 00:36:21,763
There's probably going to
be a temporal difference

721
00:36:21,763 --> 00:36:22,950
approach to doing this.

722
00:36:22,950 --> 00:36:24,620
And that's what we're
going to see now.

723
00:36:24,620 --> 00:36:26,840
So now we're going to look
into temporal difference

724
00:36:26,840 --> 00:36:30,270
methods for control.

725
00:36:30,270 --> 00:36:31,701
OK.

726
00:36:31,701 --> 00:36:33,230
So one of the
interesting things is

727
00:36:33,230 --> 00:36:37,460
that there's going to be two
different types of algorithms

728
00:36:37,460 --> 00:36:41,240
that we're going to focus on for
temporal difference for control.

729
00:36:41,240 --> 00:36:43,250
And the idea in
these settings is

730
00:36:43,250 --> 00:36:45,720
that we're going to
alternate between two steps,

731
00:36:45,720 --> 00:36:48,932
again, this policy evaluation
versus policy improvement.

732
00:36:48,932 --> 00:36:51,140
And one of the key things
to think about in this case

733
00:36:51,140 --> 00:36:52,765
is how much time are
you spending doing

734
00:36:52,765 --> 00:36:54,362
evaluation versus improvement.

735
00:36:54,362 --> 00:36:55,820
And what are we
trying to evaluate,

736
00:36:55,820 --> 00:36:58,502
and what are we improving
with respect to?

737
00:36:58,502 --> 00:36:59,960
So the idea now is
that we're going

738
00:36:59,960 --> 00:37:03,110
to compute Q pi using
temporal difference updating

739
00:37:03,110 --> 00:37:05,405
with an e-greedy policy.

740
00:37:05,405 --> 00:37:07,280
And then we're going to
do policy improvement

741
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:10,460
in the same way that we saw
before for Monte Carlo methods.

742
00:37:10,460 --> 00:37:12,530
So we can do this
e-greedy thing,

743
00:37:12,530 --> 00:37:16,160
where we are greedy with
respect to our current Q value.

744
00:37:16,160 --> 00:37:19,580
And the first algorithm we're
going to see is called SARSA.

745
00:37:19,580 --> 00:37:22,100
And the reason it is
called SARSA is it

746
00:37:22,100 --> 00:37:31,750
is state action reward
next state next action.

747
00:37:31,750 --> 00:37:34,830
It is short for that.

748
00:37:34,830 --> 00:37:37,710
S-A-R-S-A, SARSA.

749
00:37:37,710 --> 00:37:40,860
That's an easy way to think-- to
remember why this method would

750
00:37:40,860 --> 00:37:44,790
be called SARSA, because those
are the tuples we need in order

751
00:37:44,790 --> 00:37:45,640
to do updates.

752
00:37:45,640 --> 00:37:50,100
We need s, a, r, s prime,
a prime to do an update.

753
00:37:50,100 --> 00:37:52,320
And this is going to be
an on-policy algorithm.

754
00:37:52,320 --> 00:37:55,500
And this is related to what
was suggested in the back.

755
00:37:55,500 --> 00:37:57,920
Remind me your name.

756
00:37:57,920 --> 00:37:59,420
Yeah, exactly what
[INAUDIBLE] said.

757
00:37:59,420 --> 00:38:01,960
So can we also use
off-policy data?

758
00:38:01,960 --> 00:38:03,560
And we'll see that
really shortly.

759
00:38:03,560 --> 00:38:06,028
But SARSA is going
to be on-policy.

760
00:38:06,028 --> 00:38:07,570
And what we mean by
that is that it's

761
00:38:07,570 --> 00:38:09,880
going to be computing
an estimate of the Q

762
00:38:09,880 --> 00:38:13,720
value of the policy we're
using to act or what policy

763
00:38:13,720 --> 00:38:16,600
we're using to make
decisions in the world.

764
00:38:16,600 --> 00:38:18,220
So let's see how it works.

765
00:38:18,220 --> 00:38:22,720
So in general, the form
of SARSA is the following.

766
00:38:22,720 --> 00:38:25,510
We are going to
iterate, our loop

767
00:38:25,510 --> 00:38:29,120
is going to be such
that we start off.

768
00:38:29,120 --> 00:38:30,340
So this is the--

769
00:38:30,340 --> 00:38:31,400
we start in some state.

770
00:38:31,400 --> 00:38:32,210
This is the s.

771
00:38:32,210 --> 00:38:34,390
We take an action a.

772
00:38:34,390 --> 00:38:38,410
We observe reward in the
next state, and then we loop.

773
00:38:38,410 --> 00:38:40,510
And we take the
next action still

774
00:38:40,510 --> 00:38:43,923
according to the same policy.

775
00:38:43,923 --> 00:38:45,340
And then what we're
going to do is

776
00:38:45,340 --> 00:38:49,330
we're going to update our
Q-function, given this tuple

777
00:38:49,330 --> 00:38:51,470
of SARSA, essentially.

778
00:38:51,470 --> 00:38:53,290
And what we're going
to do in this case

779
00:38:53,290 --> 00:38:56,500
is going to look similar
to what we saw before.

780
00:38:56,500 --> 00:39:00,040
So we're going to
have our updated one

781
00:39:00,040 --> 00:39:06,500
is our old value plus alpha.

782
00:39:06,500 --> 00:39:08,723
So this is like our
learning rate, our target.

783
00:39:11,360 --> 00:39:17,450
s t plus 1, a t plus
1, minus Q of s t, a t.

784
00:39:21,060 --> 00:39:23,105
So this is the target.

785
00:39:26,960 --> 00:39:30,060
And it's going to look similar
to what we saw for TD 0,

786
00:39:30,060 --> 00:39:33,290
where we plug-in our immediate
reward, plus our estimate

787
00:39:33,290 --> 00:39:35,360
of the expected
discounted sum of rewards

788
00:39:35,360 --> 00:39:37,460
starting in that next state.

789
00:39:37,460 --> 00:39:40,160
And one of the important
things to notice in this case

790
00:39:40,160 --> 00:39:43,760
is we are plugging in
the actual action we

791
00:39:43,760 --> 00:39:47,020
took in the next state.

792
00:39:47,020 --> 00:39:49,018
So we're saying what is
the expected discounted

793
00:39:49,018 --> 00:39:51,560
sum of rewards starting in this
state and taking this action?

794
00:39:51,560 --> 00:39:55,330
Well, when-- estimate of
it is the immediate reward

795
00:39:55,330 --> 00:39:59,390
I got plus gamma, times the Q
value for the state I reached,

796
00:39:59,390 --> 00:40:02,415
plus the action I would
take under this policy next.

797
00:40:02,415 --> 00:40:03,790
And that's one of
the reasons why

798
00:40:03,790 --> 00:40:06,610
it's called on policy, because
it's been specific to the action

799
00:40:06,610 --> 00:40:11,120
you would actually
take under this policy.

800
00:40:11,120 --> 00:40:11,620
All right.

801
00:40:11,620 --> 00:40:13,480
And then after--
then the next thing

802
00:40:13,480 --> 00:40:15,350
we do is we do
policy improvement.

803
00:40:15,350 --> 00:40:18,580
And what we would do in this
case is, again, similar to what

804
00:40:18,580 --> 00:40:20,100
we saw, [? new one. ?]
So for all s,

805
00:40:20,100 --> 00:40:22,120
this just means for
all, for anybody

806
00:40:22,120 --> 00:40:24,880
who hasn't seen this notation.

807
00:40:24,880 --> 00:40:30,970
Pi of s is equal to
argmax over a, Q of s,

808
00:40:30,970 --> 00:40:35,385
a with probability epsilon.

809
00:40:45,834 --> 00:40:49,210
And then what we do is
we update our timestep.

810
00:40:49,210 --> 00:40:51,720
We update our epsilon.

811
00:40:51,720 --> 00:40:54,340
And then what we're going
to do is just repeat.

812
00:40:54,340 --> 00:40:56,520
So then we're going to go--

813
00:40:56,520 --> 00:40:58,535
then we're going
to go to the next--

814
00:40:58,535 --> 00:41:00,660
we're going to take our
next state, take an action,

815
00:41:00,660 --> 00:41:03,740
and repeat this updating.

816
00:41:03,740 --> 00:41:06,380
So this is called-- yeah.

817
00:41:06,380 --> 00:41:07,430
Quick question.

818
00:41:07,430 --> 00:41:12,210
Like do we-- I have a bit
confused about setting pi of s.

819
00:41:12,210 --> 00:41:15,560
Do we say pi is a
deterministic policy that

820
00:41:15,560 --> 00:41:17,745
is one of this with
this probability

821
00:41:17,745 --> 00:41:19,620
and the other one with
the other probability?

822
00:41:19,620 --> 00:41:23,150
Or are we saying it's a
stochastic policy that can--

823
00:41:23,150 --> 00:41:24,540
It's a stochastic policy.

824
00:41:24,540 --> 00:41:27,590
Yeah, so it's a
stochastic policy.

825
00:41:27,590 --> 00:41:29,397
At the very beginning,
it's totally random.

826
00:41:29,397 --> 00:41:30,980
You just take any
action in any state.

827
00:41:30,980 --> 00:41:33,892
Later, you're defining it with
respect to your current Q value.

828
00:41:33,892 --> 00:41:36,350
And you're either being greedy
with respect to that Q value

829
00:41:36,350 --> 00:41:38,150
or selecting action at random.

830
00:41:38,150 --> 00:41:39,050
Yeah.

831
00:41:39,050 --> 00:41:41,870
So one concern that
I had was that what

832
00:41:41,870 --> 00:41:45,107
if we reach a terminal
state and then just end?

833
00:41:45,107 --> 00:41:45,690
Good question.

834
00:41:45,690 --> 00:41:48,740
And this actually came up
in another conversation

835
00:41:48,740 --> 00:41:49,710
earlier this morning.

836
00:41:49,710 --> 00:41:53,490
Yes, so if you reach a terminal
state, then you just reset.

837
00:41:53,490 --> 00:42:09,180
So if s t plus 2 is terminal,
reset the episode and sample s.

838
00:42:09,180 --> 00:42:10,620
So if you ever
reach a state where

839
00:42:10,620 --> 00:42:12,180
it's terminal, what
would happen next

840
00:42:12,180 --> 00:42:14,985
is then your whole
episode just resets.

841
00:42:14,985 --> 00:42:16,860
You sample this initial
state from the world,

842
00:42:16,860 --> 00:42:17,693
and then you repeat.

843
00:42:17,693 --> 00:42:20,290
So just like if I like finished
my game, I failed at Tesla,

844
00:42:20,290 --> 00:42:22,073
it reinitializes the world.

845
00:42:22,073 --> 00:42:23,490
So these are still
sort of assumed

846
00:42:23,490 --> 00:42:25,060
to be continuing processes.

847
00:42:25,060 --> 00:42:27,150
Yeah?

848
00:42:27,150 --> 00:42:32,895
I'm wondering-- what will
[INAUDIBLE] to athletes?

849
00:42:32,895 --> 00:42:33,520
Great question.

850
00:42:33,520 --> 00:42:35,698
So what best and what
we're going to see in just

851
00:42:35,698 --> 00:42:37,740
like a slide or two-- and
you guys are-- probably

852
00:42:37,740 --> 00:42:39,610
half of you at least have
probably seen this before.

853
00:42:39,610 --> 00:42:41,568
We're going to see
Q-learning, and that's where

854
00:42:41,568 --> 00:42:43,670
it's going to be off-policy.

855
00:42:43,670 --> 00:42:44,390
OK.

856
00:42:44,390 --> 00:42:45,560
Really quick question.

857
00:42:45,560 --> 00:42:50,660
Like when it says t plus
2, do we do the step seven?

858
00:42:50,660 --> 00:42:53,580
Or do we skip it for one step
and do it in the next one,

859
00:42:53,580 --> 00:42:54,080
because--

860
00:42:54,080 --> 00:42:55,885
If it's terminal or in general?

861
00:42:55,885 --> 00:42:57,450
Like if it's terminal.

862
00:42:57,450 --> 00:43:00,470
If it's terminal,
you would halt here.

863
00:43:00,470 --> 00:43:02,267
And then you would
reset the whole thing.

864
00:43:02,267 --> 00:43:04,850
Then you would need to take an
action, observe, or next state,

865
00:43:04,850 --> 00:43:05,900
and then jump into five.

866
00:43:05,900 --> 00:43:08,010
So you have to reset to two.

867
00:43:08,010 --> 00:43:10,240
Yeah, great question.

868
00:43:10,240 --> 00:43:10,740
All right.

869
00:43:10,740 --> 00:43:11,630
So let's see.

870
00:43:11,630 --> 00:43:13,490
Well, first, let's
talk about whether this

871
00:43:13,490 --> 00:43:15,200
is guaranteed to do
anything reasonable,

872
00:43:15,200 --> 00:43:16,990
and then we'll get going.

873
00:43:16,990 --> 00:43:19,920
So I've written
this up neatly here.

874
00:43:19,920 --> 00:43:22,220
And then there's a worked
example for the Mars

875
00:43:22,220 --> 00:43:24,360
Rover at the end of the slides.

876
00:43:24,360 --> 00:43:24,860
OK.

877
00:43:24,860 --> 00:43:27,320
So one thing to
note here, too, is

878
00:43:27,320 --> 00:43:29,570
that now we've defined
a general learning rate,

879
00:43:29,570 --> 00:43:32,190
so that we have a general
learning rate here.

880
00:43:32,190 --> 00:43:32,690
OK.

881
00:43:32,690 --> 00:43:34,033
And we also have--

882
00:43:34,033 --> 00:43:35,575
let me make sure I
keep this in here.

883
00:43:38,810 --> 00:43:41,420
We're going to keep
updating our epsilon.

884
00:43:41,420 --> 00:43:45,612
OK, so is this a good approach?

885
00:43:45,612 --> 00:43:47,820
So we can think of a couple
of different things here.

886
00:43:47,820 --> 00:43:49,870
We can think of the
computational complexity.

887
00:43:49,870 --> 00:43:54,560
So here after each tuple,
we're doing an update.

888
00:43:54,560 --> 00:43:56,900
And in fact, we know that
that's in general only going

889
00:43:56,900 --> 00:44:00,307
to change the Q value for
the states and the actions

890
00:44:00,307 --> 00:44:01,140
that we're updating.

891
00:44:01,140 --> 00:44:05,010
So we just are doing that
small update each time.

892
00:44:05,010 --> 00:44:06,750
We don't have to sum
over all the states.

893
00:44:06,750 --> 00:44:08,833
So there's nothing that
depends on the state space

894
00:44:08,833 --> 00:44:10,620
size per update.

895
00:44:10,620 --> 00:44:13,010
But of course, we're doing
this many, many, many times.

896
00:44:13,010 --> 00:44:15,520
Does this converge to
the optimal Q-function?

897
00:44:15,520 --> 00:44:17,390
So what we have
here in this case is

898
00:44:17,390 --> 00:44:19,790
we have this
weighted combination

899
00:44:19,790 --> 00:44:24,380
between our last Q-function
and this new target.

900
00:44:24,380 --> 00:44:27,560
And again, Q is an
estimate of the performance

901
00:44:27,560 --> 00:44:30,780
of a policy that might be
changing at each time point.

902
00:44:30,780 --> 00:44:32,290
So it's similar to Monte Carlo.

903
00:44:32,290 --> 00:44:33,900
Like we're just like
we're constantly

904
00:44:33,900 --> 00:44:35,980
changing the policy
in this case.

905
00:44:35,980 --> 00:44:38,550
And so that should feel
a little bit concerning.

906
00:44:38,550 --> 00:44:40,300
And empirically, it
often does quite well.

907
00:44:40,300 --> 00:44:42,700
But Q-learning is more popular.

908
00:44:42,700 --> 00:44:45,290
OK, so what are the
convergence properties?

909
00:44:45,290 --> 00:44:46,840
So it turns out that
in terms of some

910
00:44:46,840 --> 00:44:48,290
of the mathematical
formulations,

911
00:44:48,290 --> 00:44:51,640
this relates really strongly
to stochastic approximation.

912
00:44:51,640 --> 00:44:53,980
And this is a deep
literature with lots

913
00:44:53,980 --> 00:44:55,570
of really amazing results.

914
00:44:55,570 --> 00:44:59,180
In the finite state
and finite action case,

915
00:44:59,180 --> 00:45:03,130
it's going to converge to
the optimal Q star for SARSA,

916
00:45:03,130 --> 00:45:06,553
if your policy sequence
satisfies the condition of GLIE.

917
00:45:06,553 --> 00:45:08,470
So we're going to visit
all states and actions

918
00:45:08,470 --> 00:45:09,860
an infinite number of times.

919
00:45:09,860 --> 00:45:12,640
And we're getting greedy
and greedier over time.

920
00:45:12,640 --> 00:45:15,490
And we have to put in a
condition about the learning

921
00:45:15,490 --> 00:45:17,560
rates, the step sizes.

922
00:45:17,560 --> 00:45:19,390
So in particular,
they have to satisfy

923
00:45:19,390 --> 00:45:22,100
the Robbins-Munro sequence.

924
00:45:22,100 --> 00:45:24,650
So they have to satisfy
these two things, which

925
00:45:24,650 --> 00:45:26,990
is their sum goes to
infinity, and their square

926
00:45:26,990 --> 00:45:28,250
is less than infinity.

927
00:45:28,250 --> 00:45:30,800
And we've seen this before.

928
00:45:30,800 --> 00:45:33,950
And an example of this
would be a t equals 1 over t

929
00:45:33,950 --> 00:45:35,640
satisfies these conditions.

930
00:45:38,570 --> 00:45:43,060
So these results
really sort of rely

931
00:45:43,060 --> 00:45:45,875
on these really nice results
from stochastic approximation,

932
00:45:45,875 --> 00:45:47,750
because it should be a
little bit surprising.

933
00:45:47,750 --> 00:45:49,417
You can think of this
as kind of there's

934
00:45:49,417 --> 00:45:52,270
these different-- there's
these mixing processes that

935
00:45:52,270 --> 00:45:54,440
are going on, because
our policy is changing,

936
00:45:54,440 --> 00:45:55,733
our estimates are changing.

937
00:45:55,733 --> 00:45:57,400
How can we be sure
that it's essentially

938
00:45:57,400 --> 00:45:59,890
going to be stable enough
that over time, we're

939
00:45:59,890 --> 00:46:03,140
actually going to converge to
something that's both fixed--

940
00:46:03,140 --> 00:46:05,140
like we're not just going
to oscillate forever--

941
00:46:05,140 --> 00:46:07,420
and that it is optimal?

942
00:46:07,420 --> 00:46:12,340
So it should not be at all clear
why this would necessarily work.

943
00:46:12,340 --> 00:46:17,290
And this is where we rely on
those results from stochastic

944
00:46:17,290 --> 00:46:19,990
approximation, that also had to
be extended to think about these

945
00:46:19,990 --> 00:46:23,110
particular cases during a number
of really beautiful papers from

946
00:46:23,110 --> 00:46:25,300
the 1990s.

947
00:46:25,300 --> 00:46:34,690
So those are the 1992 and
1994 papers that show this.

948
00:46:34,690 --> 00:46:36,660
OK, so there's some
really cool results

949
00:46:36,660 --> 00:46:39,240
that illustrate why
this is possible.

950
00:46:39,240 --> 00:46:43,890
OK, SARSA for tabular settings
under some mild conditions

951
00:46:43,890 --> 00:46:46,300
is guaranteed to
converge to Q star.

952
00:46:46,300 --> 00:46:49,410
So now let's see if we can
do off-policy learning.

953
00:46:49,410 --> 00:46:51,073
So off-policy
learning is the idea

954
00:46:51,073 --> 00:46:53,490
that now we're going to be
trying to estimate and evaluate

955
00:46:53,490 --> 00:46:56,010
a policy using experience
gathered from following

956
00:46:56,010 --> 00:46:57,950
a different policy.

957
00:46:57,950 --> 00:47:00,800
So, so far, we've been thinking
about Monte Carlo methods

958
00:47:00,800 --> 00:47:02,630
and SARSA, where
we're at least sort

959
00:47:02,630 --> 00:47:05,330
of trying to always approximate
the value of the most

960
00:47:05,330 --> 00:47:08,460
recent policy or averaged
over all those policies.

961
00:47:08,460 --> 00:47:09,980
But now we're
going to explicitly

962
00:47:09,980 --> 00:47:14,390
be trying to estimate Q
star at all time points.

963
00:47:14,390 --> 00:47:15,662
OK.

964
00:47:15,662 --> 00:47:20,710
So in Q-learning, we are going
to try to directly estimate

965
00:47:20,710 --> 00:47:22,057
the value of pi star--

966
00:47:22,057 --> 00:47:23,890
which remember, we don't
know, because if we

967
00:47:23,890 --> 00:47:25,300
knew what pi star
was, then we wouldn't

968
00:47:25,300 --> 00:47:26,890
have to do any of
this learning--

969
00:47:26,890 --> 00:47:28,670
with another behavior policy.

970
00:47:28,670 --> 00:47:29,345
Pi b.

971
00:47:29,345 --> 00:47:30,970
So we're going to be
acting in one way.

972
00:47:30,970 --> 00:47:32,428
And we're going to
be trying to use

973
00:47:32,428 --> 00:47:36,010
that data to estimate the
value of an alternative policy.

974
00:47:36,010 --> 00:47:39,540
And that's what Q-learning does.

975
00:47:39,540 --> 00:47:42,120
So in Q-learning,
the key difference

976
00:47:42,120 --> 00:47:44,340
is that instead of trying
to think about what

977
00:47:44,340 --> 00:47:47,350
is the action we actually
took on the next time step,

978
00:47:47,350 --> 00:47:50,160
we're just going to figure out
what is the best action I could

979
00:47:50,160 --> 00:47:54,670
have taken, because we
know for the Q star value,

980
00:47:54,670 --> 00:47:58,170
it is the estimate of the
optimal expected reward

981
00:47:58,170 --> 00:48:00,510
you could get if you
take the current action

982
00:48:00,510 --> 00:48:03,380
and then act
optimally from now on.

983
00:48:03,380 --> 00:48:04,880
So really, you
would normally like

984
00:48:04,880 --> 00:48:06,150
to have something like this.

985
00:48:06,150 --> 00:48:09,950
So sum over s prime probability
of s prime given s, a,

986
00:48:09,950 --> 00:48:13,340
times V star of s prime.

987
00:48:13,340 --> 00:48:15,900
So you would have in
the Bellman equation.

988
00:48:15,900 --> 00:48:18,470
And what we're going to do
here, what Q-learning does

989
00:48:18,470 --> 00:48:24,200
is it approximates
that by this max.

990
00:48:24,200 --> 00:48:26,840
And that is different
than what SARSA does,

991
00:48:26,840 --> 00:48:30,862
because SARSA used
the actual action.

992
00:48:30,862 --> 00:48:32,320
And Q-learning
says, I don't really

993
00:48:32,320 --> 00:48:33,860
care what actual
action you took.

994
00:48:33,860 --> 00:48:35,930
I care about what is the best
thing you could have done there,

995
00:48:35,930 --> 00:48:37,305
because that's
giving me a better

996
00:48:37,305 --> 00:48:39,850
estimate of the maximum expected
discounted sum of rewards

997
00:48:39,850 --> 00:48:44,720
I'd get from that state
till the end of time.

998
00:48:44,720 --> 00:48:46,860
So that is what
Q-learning is doing.

999
00:48:46,860 --> 00:48:49,080
So it looks really similar
to the SARSA update.

1000
00:48:49,080 --> 00:48:51,750
But our target is going
to be the reward I got,

1001
00:48:51,750 --> 00:48:54,335
plus the best reward that I
think I could have achieved

1002
00:48:54,335 --> 00:48:55,210
from that next state.

1003
00:48:58,980 --> 00:48:59,480
All right.

1004
00:48:59,480 --> 00:49:02,050
So then we get an algorithm
that looks extremely

1005
00:49:02,050 --> 00:49:03,740
similar to what we saw before.

1006
00:49:03,740 --> 00:49:07,790
But we have this max
over the next action.

1007
00:49:07,790 --> 00:49:09,040
And then I'll just make sure--

1008
00:49:09,040 --> 00:49:10,674
I think I forgot
to write down here.

1009
00:49:14,550 --> 00:49:17,910
So whether we're doing Monte
Carlo or SARSA or Q-learning,

1010
00:49:17,910 --> 00:49:20,790
in all of these cases, we're
interleaving gathering some data

1011
00:49:20,790 --> 00:49:23,070
under our current
epsilon greedy policy,

1012
00:49:23,070 --> 00:49:25,410
and then using it
to update a Q value.

1013
00:49:25,410 --> 00:49:28,660
And because we don't know
what the actual Q-function is,

1014
00:49:28,660 --> 00:49:30,990
we're sort of doing this
weighted approximation

1015
00:49:30,990 --> 00:49:35,580
between our current estimate of
the Q-function and the target

1016
00:49:35,580 --> 00:49:37,350
that we just put in.

1017
00:49:37,350 --> 00:49:39,305
And we do this over and
over and over again.

1018
00:49:43,210 --> 00:49:45,460
So similar to SARSA,
the conditions

1019
00:49:45,460 --> 00:49:47,920
to make sure that Q-learning
in the tabular case--

1020
00:49:47,920 --> 00:49:50,140
so things get a lot
more complicated

1021
00:49:50,140 --> 00:49:55,380
once we go into the
function approximation case.

1022
00:49:55,380 --> 00:49:58,310
But in order for
tabular Q-learning

1023
00:49:58,310 --> 00:50:01,153
with e-greedy exploration to
converge the optimal Q star,

1024
00:50:01,153 --> 00:50:03,320
you again need to visit
everything infinitely often.

1025
00:50:03,320 --> 00:50:07,730
Your step sizes has to satisfy
the Robbins-Munro sequence.

1026
00:50:07,730 --> 00:50:11,900
And one important
thing to notice here

1027
00:50:11,900 --> 00:50:20,370
is that you can estimate Q
star without being GLIE, which

1028
00:50:20,370 --> 00:50:26,240
is different than SARSA, because
you're always doing this max.

1029
00:50:26,240 --> 00:50:29,700
So even if you act
completely randomly,

1030
00:50:29,700 --> 00:50:32,610
so just like infinite
exploration, not being greedy,

1031
00:50:32,610 --> 00:50:38,390
you could learn Q star, because
in your Q star estimate here,

1032
00:50:38,390 --> 00:50:41,554
you're always doing this max a.

1033
00:50:41,554 --> 00:50:46,320
So that's an important
difference compared to SARSA.

1034
00:50:46,320 --> 00:50:48,320
But if you actually want
to use that information

1035
00:50:48,320 --> 00:50:49,830
to make good decisions
in the world,

1036
00:50:49,830 --> 00:50:51,290
you need to become
greedy over time

1037
00:50:51,290 --> 00:50:54,470
and be using that information to
actually select the best action

1038
00:50:54,470 --> 00:50:57,130
according to your Q-function.

1039
00:50:57,130 --> 00:50:59,770
And for e-greedy
algorithms with Q-learning,

1040
00:50:59,770 --> 00:51:01,790
you normally do k over
epsilon over time.

1041
00:51:01,790 --> 00:51:03,790
So you're getting more
and more deterministic.

1042
00:51:03,790 --> 00:51:06,070
And you're taking your
estimate of what Q star is

1043
00:51:06,070 --> 00:51:07,940
and using it to make decisions.

1044
00:51:11,667 --> 00:51:14,000
Yeah, we're now going to go
into function approximation.

1045
00:51:14,000 --> 00:51:16,625
I'm just going to pause there in
case people had any questions.

1046
00:51:21,600 --> 00:51:23,650
Yeah.

1047
00:51:23,650 --> 00:51:26,830
So for either using
SARSA or Q-learning,

1048
00:51:26,830 --> 00:51:28,740
will it converge--
does it converge

1049
00:51:28,740 --> 00:51:32,720
to a stochastic policy or
a deterministic policy?

1050
00:51:32,720 --> 00:51:34,110
Great, great question.

1051
00:51:34,110 --> 00:51:37,410
So if there are no ties
in your Q-function,

1052
00:51:37,410 --> 00:51:40,320
as in like for any action
there or any state,

1053
00:51:40,320 --> 00:51:42,080
there is a uniquely
best action, it'll

1054
00:51:42,080 --> 00:51:45,020
converge to a
deterministic policy.

1055
00:51:45,020 --> 00:51:47,120
If there are ties,
it'll generally

1056
00:51:47,120 --> 00:51:48,600
pick between those arbitrarily.

1057
00:51:48,600 --> 00:51:50,683
There'll be an infinite
number of optimal policies

1058
00:51:50,683 --> 00:51:53,175
if there are ties
in your Q-function.

1059
00:51:53,175 --> 00:51:53,800
Great question.

1060
00:51:58,310 --> 00:51:58,830
All right.

1061
00:51:58,830 --> 00:51:59,750
So now what we're
going to do is we're

1062
00:51:59,750 --> 00:52:01,830
going to layer on function
approximation on top.

1063
00:52:01,830 --> 00:52:03,530
So this was all
assuming that we just

1064
00:52:03,530 --> 00:52:05,480
had this table where
you could write down

1065
00:52:05,480 --> 00:52:08,610
the value for every state
and action separately.

1066
00:52:08,610 --> 00:52:10,770
And now we want to use
function approximation.

1067
00:52:10,770 --> 00:52:14,690
So we can start to do
problems like Atari.

1068
00:52:14,690 --> 00:52:16,835
So the motivation for
doing this-- and I

1069
00:52:16,835 --> 00:52:19,013
know for those of you who've
taken machine learning,

1070
00:52:19,013 --> 00:52:20,930
this is probably clear,
but it's nice to think

1071
00:52:20,930 --> 00:52:23,138
about what this means in
the context of reinforcement

1072
00:52:23,138 --> 00:52:23,730
learning.

1073
00:52:23,730 --> 00:52:25,880
So what are the things
that we might be storing

1074
00:52:25,880 --> 00:52:27,410
or trying to manipulate?

1075
00:52:27,410 --> 00:52:29,930
That might be the dynamics
or reward model, the value

1076
00:52:29,930 --> 00:52:33,260
function, the state action
value function, or the policy.

1077
00:52:33,260 --> 00:52:35,550
And if you were thinking
about pixel space,

1078
00:52:35,550 --> 00:52:39,530
you do not want to write that
down as like one different value

1079
00:52:39,530 --> 00:52:42,650
for every-- bless you-- for
every different possible image

1080
00:52:42,650 --> 00:52:43,820
in the world.

1081
00:52:43,820 --> 00:52:46,220
So we're going to want compact
representations like what

1082
00:52:46,220 --> 00:52:49,580
we can do with neural networks,
so that we reduce the memory we

1083
00:52:49,580 --> 00:52:52,190
need to write down those
dynamics models, the value

1084
00:52:52,190 --> 00:52:54,120
function, or Q or the policy.

1085
00:52:54,120 --> 00:52:55,590
We reduce the computation.

1086
00:52:55,590 --> 00:52:59,875
And ideally, we might even be
able to reduce the experience.

1087
00:52:59,875 --> 00:53:01,250
And I think this
last point maybe

1088
00:53:01,250 --> 00:53:03,292
is a particularly interesting
one to think about.

1089
00:53:03,292 --> 00:53:05,480
So you can imagine, if
your agent is learning

1090
00:53:05,480 --> 00:53:08,910
to play an Atari game
or play Breakout,

1091
00:53:08,910 --> 00:53:11,830
it might want to know that,
oh, well, if these pixels are

1092
00:53:11,830 --> 00:53:13,580
slightly different
here, most of the time,

1093
00:53:13,580 --> 00:53:15,830
you might still take
the same decision.

1094
00:53:15,830 --> 00:53:18,230
And so then instead of
having to learn from scratch

1095
00:53:18,230 --> 00:53:19,790
what to do in each
state, you can get

1096
00:53:19,790 --> 00:53:21,410
this sort of generalization.

1097
00:53:21,410 --> 00:53:23,480
And that could be really
important in terms

1098
00:53:23,480 --> 00:53:24,890
of reducing the
amount of data we

1099
00:53:24,890 --> 00:53:29,120
need to learn to
make good decisions.

1100
00:53:29,120 --> 00:53:29,670
All right.

1101
00:53:29,670 --> 00:53:31,707
So how do we do this?

1102
00:53:31,707 --> 00:53:33,290
What we're going to
try to do is we're

1103
00:53:33,290 --> 00:53:35,790
going to essentially do the same
thing as what we did before.

1104
00:53:35,790 --> 00:53:38,040
But we're also going to have
to incorporate a function

1105
00:53:38,040 --> 00:53:39,023
approximation step.

1106
00:53:39,023 --> 00:53:40,940
So let's just think about
how we would do this

1107
00:53:40,940 --> 00:53:42,190
if we had an Oracle.

1108
00:53:42,190 --> 00:53:44,600
So what I mean by
this is we're not

1109
00:53:44,600 --> 00:53:47,020
thinking yet right now
about all the learning

1110
00:53:47,020 --> 00:53:48,020
and like gathering data.

1111
00:53:48,020 --> 00:53:50,395
We're just assuming how do we
fit a function to represent

1112
00:53:50,395 --> 00:53:52,220
our Q-function.

1113
00:53:52,220 --> 00:53:55,070
So let's imagine that you had
an Oracle that for any state

1114
00:53:55,070 --> 00:53:57,260
and action, it would
give you the true value

1115
00:53:57,260 --> 00:54:00,290
for a particular policy
and that state and action.

1116
00:54:00,290 --> 00:54:03,710
So it would tell you like
that's three or that's seven.

1117
00:54:03,710 --> 00:54:05,570
So then you could
say, OK, now I've just

1118
00:54:05,570 --> 00:54:07,290
got a supervised
learning problem,

1119
00:54:07,290 --> 00:54:09,680
I've got input tuples
of states and actions.

1120
00:54:09,680 --> 00:54:12,800
And I have output
values of my Q-function.

1121
00:54:12,800 --> 00:54:16,160
And what I want to do now is
just learn a function to--

1122
00:54:16,160 --> 00:54:18,690
a regression function to say,
given the state and action,

1123
00:54:18,690 --> 00:54:20,990
what is the output?

1124
00:54:20,990 --> 00:54:23,330
So imagine that
you're in a case where

1125
00:54:23,330 --> 00:54:25,490
we have a continuous
set of states,

1126
00:54:25,490 --> 00:54:28,040
and we only have one action.

1127
00:54:28,040 --> 00:54:32,660
Then you might just have
all these different points.

1128
00:54:32,660 --> 00:54:35,120
And maybe you just want to
learn a function that predicts

1129
00:54:35,120 --> 00:54:39,580
the Q for every single state.

1130
00:54:39,580 --> 00:54:41,690
And you just learn like
a parametric function.

1131
00:54:41,690 --> 00:54:44,020
Or it could be a
deep neural network.

1132
00:54:44,020 --> 00:54:46,100
And in general, just like
in supervised learning,

1133
00:54:46,100 --> 00:54:47,975
the objective is going
to be to find the best

1134
00:54:47,975 --> 00:54:50,980
approximate representation
of Q, given some weights

1135
00:54:50,980 --> 00:54:53,950
or given some neural
network architecture.

1136
00:54:53,950 --> 00:54:56,355
So we've got some neural net.

1137
00:54:58,860 --> 00:55:01,310
And we're just going
to fit this to try

1138
00:55:01,310 --> 00:55:04,040
to-- if we had these points.

1139
00:55:04,040 --> 00:55:05,910
But of course, we don't
have these points.

1140
00:55:05,910 --> 00:55:07,620
And we're going to see how
we're going to handle it.

1141
00:55:07,620 --> 00:55:08,430
We don't have these points.

1142
00:55:08,430 --> 00:55:10,763
But this is the intuition is
that if you had these, then

1143
00:55:10,763 --> 00:55:14,340
you could do the function
approximation step by saying,

1144
00:55:14,340 --> 00:55:15,373
OK, well, how do I--

1145
00:55:15,373 --> 00:55:16,790
I'm going to handle
generalization

1146
00:55:16,790 --> 00:55:19,800
by using a linear function or
a deep neural network to say,

1147
00:55:19,800 --> 00:55:22,255
for each of these states and
actions, what is the output?

1148
00:55:25,040 --> 00:55:27,652
So just to highlight
here in this class,

1149
00:55:27,652 --> 00:55:29,360
generally, we will be
focusing on methods

1150
00:55:29,360 --> 00:55:31,280
that use stochastic
gradient descent to try

1151
00:55:31,280 --> 00:55:32,760
to fit these functions.

1152
00:55:32,760 --> 00:55:35,147
And again, I expect most of
this is familiar for you guys

1153
00:55:35,147 --> 00:55:36,480
if you've done machine learning.

1154
00:55:36,480 --> 00:55:41,390
If you haven't, you can come
talk to me or any of the TAs.

1155
00:55:41,390 --> 00:55:43,670
Generally, we're going to
just use mean squared error.

1156
00:55:43,670 --> 00:55:47,000
And we're going to try to
fit a function that minimizes

1157
00:55:47,000 --> 00:55:48,180
the mean squared error.

1158
00:55:48,180 --> 00:55:50,720
We're going to do gradient
descent to find a local minimum.

1159
00:55:50,720 --> 00:55:54,290
And we're going to do
stochastic gradient descent just

1160
00:55:54,290 --> 00:55:58,930
to compute an
approximate gradient.

1161
00:55:58,930 --> 00:56:00,010
Right.

1162
00:56:00,010 --> 00:56:04,110
So in this case--

1163
00:56:04,110 --> 00:56:07,032
and I have here is just to
write that out really quickly.

1164
00:56:07,032 --> 00:56:08,490
You would have
something like this.

1165
00:56:08,490 --> 00:56:09,810
You'd have w, J, and--

1166
00:56:12,960 --> 00:56:14,420
it's just the
derivative of this.

1167
00:56:17,320 --> 00:56:17,900
I'm sorry.

1168
00:56:17,900 --> 00:56:18,500
Like that.

1169
00:56:21,460 --> 00:56:23,440
So I'm going to take
this equation star.

1170
00:56:23,440 --> 00:56:25,720
And I'm just going to take
the derivative of it, which

1171
00:56:25,720 --> 00:56:29,100
is going to be two.

1172
00:56:48,125 --> 00:56:50,250
So we're just going to take
the derivative of this.

1173
00:56:50,250 --> 00:56:51,320
And essentially,
that just means we're

1174
00:56:51,320 --> 00:56:52,820
going to have to
take the derivative

1175
00:56:52,820 --> 00:56:54,930
through our Q-function
representation,

1176
00:56:54,930 --> 00:56:58,130
like using autodiff for
deep neural networks.

1177
00:56:58,130 --> 00:57:02,367
And then we can use this
to update our weights.

1178
00:57:02,367 --> 00:57:04,450
All right, so we'll do
stochastic gradient descent

1179
00:57:04,450 --> 00:57:06,040
to do this.

1180
00:57:06,040 --> 00:57:09,350
And the main thing
is that that's

1181
00:57:09,350 --> 00:57:12,590
what we're going to be doing to
plug-in in order to do policy

1182
00:57:12,590 --> 00:57:16,020
evaluation or to do control.

1183
00:57:16,020 --> 00:57:17,980
So of course, in general,
we don't have those.

1184
00:57:17,980 --> 00:57:21,190
We don't have for each state and
action, what the Q value was.

1185
00:57:21,190 --> 00:57:23,470
If it was, we wouldn't
need to do any learning.

1186
00:57:23,470 --> 00:57:25,360
We need to learn that from data.

1187
00:57:25,360 --> 00:57:27,810
And so the idea is
that we're going

1188
00:57:27,810 --> 00:57:31,380
to do model-free state action,
value function approximation.

1189
00:57:31,380 --> 00:57:33,580
So just like what we've
been seeing before,

1190
00:57:33,580 --> 00:57:37,365
we're doing model-free
state action value function.

1191
00:57:37,365 --> 00:57:39,240
Now we're going to
actually do that, but just

1192
00:57:39,240 --> 00:57:41,400
do an approximation, where
instead of writing it down

1193
00:57:41,400 --> 00:57:43,983
as a table, we're going to write
it down with these parameters

1194
00:57:43,983 --> 00:57:46,770
function--
parametrized functions.

1195
00:57:46,770 --> 00:57:52,158
OK, so the idea now
is like similarly,

1196
00:57:52,158 --> 00:57:53,700
we just saw before
all these methods,

1197
00:57:53,700 --> 00:57:56,490
either where we use Monte Carlo
methods or temporal difference

1198
00:57:56,490 --> 00:57:59,700
methods to try to do
these approximations.

1199
00:57:59,700 --> 00:58:02,280
Now what we're going to do is
that when we do the estimate

1200
00:58:02,280 --> 00:58:06,790
update step, we're also going to
fit the function approximator.

1201
00:58:06,790 --> 00:58:08,170
So just like in
the algorithms we

1202
00:58:08,170 --> 00:58:10,690
saw before where we do like
policy evaluation and policy

1203
00:58:10,690 --> 00:58:13,250
improvement, now when we
do the policy evaluation,

1204
00:58:13,250 --> 00:58:16,130
we're also going to just refit
like our whole Q-function,

1205
00:58:16,130 --> 00:58:18,890
for example.

1206
00:58:18,890 --> 00:58:21,000
OK, so let's see
how that could work.

1207
00:58:21,000 --> 00:58:24,450
So for Monte Carlo value
function approximation,

1208
00:58:24,450 --> 00:58:26,150
we're going to remember
that our return

1209
00:58:26,150 --> 00:58:30,260
G is an unbiased but noisy
sample of the expected return.

1210
00:58:30,260 --> 00:58:34,350
So we can think of us having
this state, action return,

1211
00:58:34,350 --> 00:58:36,590
state, action,
return, et cetera.

1212
00:58:36,590 --> 00:58:41,570
And so you can substitute in
those Gs for the true Q pi

1213
00:58:41,570 --> 00:58:44,450
when you're doing your fitting.

1214
00:58:44,450 --> 00:58:47,450
So let's see what
that would look like.

1215
00:58:47,450 --> 00:58:51,540
So in this case, remember
what you would like here

1216
00:58:51,540 --> 00:58:53,400
when we're doing our
function approximation

1217
00:58:53,400 --> 00:58:57,370
is that this is the
real Q of the policy,

1218
00:58:57,370 --> 00:58:59,950
but we don't know what the
real policy, real Q value is.

1219
00:58:59,950 --> 00:59:04,270
So we're going to plug-in
r, observed return.

1220
00:59:04,270 --> 00:59:14,290
So, we want-- would
like Q of s, a.

1221
00:59:14,290 --> 00:59:15,500
But we don't have that.

1222
00:59:15,500 --> 00:59:18,730
So we're going to plug-in the
return that we just observed.

1223
00:59:18,730 --> 00:59:20,540
And then we'll just
do the derivative--

1224
00:59:20,540 --> 00:59:22,415
we'll be plugging that
in for our derivative.

1225
00:59:22,415 --> 00:59:24,070
And then update
our weights using

1226
00:59:24,070 --> 00:59:26,530
that derivative with
respect to minimizing

1227
00:59:26,530 --> 00:59:29,100
the mean squared error.

1228
00:59:29,100 --> 00:59:32,020
OK, so this would just
be for policy evaluation.

1229
00:59:32,020 --> 00:59:34,320
If you have a fixed
policy, you would just

1230
00:59:34,320 --> 00:59:35,710
do this at each time point.

1231
00:59:35,710 --> 00:59:37,500
So after you see--

1232
00:59:37,500 --> 00:59:40,670
after you get a return, then you
would update your Q-function.

1233
00:59:40,670 --> 00:59:43,147
And you would do this
many, many times.

1234
00:59:43,147 --> 00:59:45,480
And for some of you, this
might start to look redundant.

1235
00:59:45,480 --> 00:59:46,580
But I think it's
just useful to see

1236
00:59:46,580 --> 00:59:48,997
that essentially the structure
of all of these algorithms,

1237
00:59:48,997 --> 00:59:52,460
whether it is policy evaluation
or tabular or function

1238
00:59:52,460 --> 00:59:54,900
approximation, is
extremely similar.

1239
00:59:54,900 --> 00:59:58,820
We are just either sampling an
episode or sampling a tuple.

1240
00:59:58,820 --> 01:00:00,440
We are going to
do one step, which

1241
01:00:00,440 --> 01:00:02,360
is like policy evaluation,
where we update

1242
01:00:02,360 --> 01:00:05,150
our estimate of the
Q-function, maybe optionally do

1243
01:00:05,150 --> 01:00:07,080
function approximation fitting.

1244
01:00:07,080 --> 01:00:10,730
And then we're going to use that
to figure out how to act next

1245
01:00:10,730 --> 01:00:12,580
if we are doing control.

1246
01:00:12,580 --> 01:00:15,110
We'll see an example
of that shortly.

1247
01:00:15,110 --> 01:00:18,490
OK, so that is Monte Carlo.

1248
01:00:18,490 --> 01:00:18,990
OK.

1249
01:00:18,990 --> 01:00:19,490
Oops.

1250
01:00:24,080 --> 01:00:24,880
OK.

1251
01:00:24,880 --> 01:00:25,380
All right.

1252
01:00:25,380 --> 01:00:27,630
For temporal difference
learning, it's very similar.

1253
01:00:27,630 --> 01:00:31,370
But now we are going to have
this weighted sum where we

1254
01:00:31,370 --> 01:00:33,050
plug-in--

1255
01:00:33,050 --> 01:00:34,140
we bootstrap.

1256
01:00:34,140 --> 01:00:36,500
So we plug-in our current
estimate of the value of s

1257
01:00:36,500 --> 01:00:37,550
prime.

1258
01:00:37,550 --> 01:00:39,840
So this is the same
update we saw before.

1259
01:00:39,840 --> 01:00:42,410
This was for tabular
cases and now we're

1260
01:00:42,410 --> 01:00:45,920
going to do it for
function approximation.

1261
01:00:45,920 --> 01:00:47,291
OK.

1262
01:00:47,291 --> 01:00:48,875
So let's first just
see how we do it

1263
01:00:48,875 --> 01:00:50,000
for function approximation.

1264
01:00:50,000 --> 01:00:52,000
It's just useful, I think,
when we look at this,

1265
01:00:52,000 --> 01:00:54,740
to think about all the different
ways we're doing approximations.

1266
01:00:54,740 --> 01:00:57,470
We are sampling to
approximate the expected

1267
01:00:57,470 --> 01:00:58,830
value over the next state.

1268
01:00:58,830 --> 01:01:01,940
We are bootstrapping to plug-in
what the value of those states

1269
01:01:01,940 --> 01:01:02,510
are.

1270
01:01:02,510 --> 01:01:04,760
And now we're also going to
do function approximation,

1271
01:01:04,760 --> 01:01:07,100
because we're going to represent
the value of a function

1272
01:01:07,100 --> 01:01:08,340
with some weights.

1273
01:01:08,340 --> 01:01:08,840
OK.

1274
01:01:08,840 --> 01:01:11,620
So we're going to
have these weights.

1275
01:01:11,620 --> 01:01:13,920
All right.

1276
01:01:13,920 --> 01:01:17,040
And again, we can just do
stochastic gradient descent

1277
01:01:17,040 --> 01:01:19,865
to fit our weight function to
represent that value function.

1278
01:01:22,568 --> 01:01:23,068
OK.

1279
01:01:27,303 --> 01:01:28,720
So you'll get
something like this,

1280
01:01:28,720 --> 01:01:31,490
where as long as if you're
in a terminal state,

1281
01:01:31,490 --> 01:01:32,950
you'll restart the episode.

1282
01:01:32,950 --> 01:01:35,890
Otherwise, you'll just be doing
these computing the gradient

1283
01:01:35,890 --> 01:01:38,682
with respect to your minimizing
your mean squared error

1284
01:01:38,682 --> 01:01:39,765
and updating your weights.

1285
01:01:44,660 --> 01:01:49,840
And then we'll see how
we do this for control.

1286
01:01:49,840 --> 01:01:53,850
So for control, it's
going to be very similar.

1287
01:01:53,850 --> 01:01:55,380
Now, what we'll
make sure to do is

1288
01:01:55,380 --> 01:01:57,660
we're always going to be
using the Q-function instead

1289
01:01:57,660 --> 01:01:59,280
of the value function.

1290
01:01:59,280 --> 01:02:03,190
And we're now often going to be
doing off-policy learning again,

1291
01:02:03,190 --> 01:02:05,320
like Q-learning.

1292
01:02:05,320 --> 01:02:08,128
So we'll again do
stochastic gradient descent.

1293
01:02:08,128 --> 01:02:09,670
With respect to our
Q-function, we're

1294
01:02:09,670 --> 01:02:11,240
going to sample the gradient.

1295
01:02:11,240 --> 01:02:12,430
And we'll have very--

1296
01:02:12,430 --> 01:02:16,030
an algorithm that's very similar
to the one we've seen before.

1297
01:02:16,030 --> 01:02:22,240
So we can either use SARSA where
we have our Q-function, where

1298
01:02:22,240 --> 01:02:25,250
we always plug-in what is the
actual action we took next.

1299
01:02:25,250 --> 01:02:28,780
Or we can have Q-learning
where we plug-in

1300
01:02:28,780 --> 01:02:33,440
a max over the next Q-function.

1301
01:02:33,440 --> 01:02:35,960
And raise your hand if you've
implemented deep Q-learning

1302
01:02:35,960 --> 01:02:37,480
before.

1303
01:02:37,480 --> 01:02:38,240
OK.

1304
01:02:38,240 --> 01:02:39,950
So one person, but
most people not.

1305
01:02:39,950 --> 01:02:40,470
Yeah.

1306
01:02:40,470 --> 01:02:40,970
OK.

1307
01:02:40,970 --> 01:02:43,387
So you can imagine in general
this is any form of function

1308
01:02:43,387 --> 01:02:44,010
approximator.

1309
01:02:44,010 --> 01:02:48,670
But often this is going to be
like a deep neural network.

1310
01:02:48,670 --> 01:02:50,140
OK.

1311
01:02:50,140 --> 01:02:53,630
Now, one thing I just want to
highlight here is that, again,

1312
01:02:53,630 --> 01:02:56,110
just being in terms of being
concerned whether all of this

1313
01:02:56,110 --> 01:02:58,960
is going to work, there's
a lot of approximations

1314
01:02:58,960 --> 01:03:01,600
that are happening here, so
particularly for Q-learning.

1315
01:03:01,600 --> 01:03:05,290
And it's led to what Sutton
and Barto, the authors

1316
01:03:05,290 --> 01:03:07,690
of the book that is the
optional textbook for the class

1317
01:03:07,690 --> 01:03:10,120
called The Deadly Triad.

1318
01:03:10,120 --> 01:03:13,150
And what they say is that if you
are doing bootstrapping, meaning

1319
01:03:13,150 --> 01:03:15,130
that you're plugging
in an estimate of what

1320
01:03:15,130 --> 01:03:18,130
is the value of the next state
and you're doing function

1321
01:03:18,130 --> 01:03:20,530
approximation, like you're
using a deep neural network

1322
01:03:20,530 --> 01:03:24,062
or a linear function, and you're
doing off-policy learning where

1323
01:03:24,062 --> 01:03:26,020
you are acting in a
different way than the data

1324
01:03:26,020 --> 01:03:28,340
you're getting,
under those cases,

1325
01:03:28,340 --> 01:03:31,320
you may not converge at all.

1326
01:03:31,320 --> 01:03:33,790
Like just your
Q-function may oscillate.

1327
01:03:33,790 --> 01:03:36,330
You may not converge
to anything.

1328
01:03:36,330 --> 01:03:38,430
And you are certainly
not guaranteed

1329
01:03:38,430 --> 01:03:41,590
to converge to Q star.

1330
01:03:41,590 --> 01:03:46,510
So it's just good to keep in
mind that that could occur.

1331
01:03:46,510 --> 01:03:49,220
I think for some intuition
for why this can occur--

1332
01:03:49,220 --> 01:03:50,950
the Bellman operator,
if you think back

1333
01:03:50,950 --> 01:03:53,780
a couple of lectures ago,
we proved as a contraction,

1334
01:03:53,780 --> 01:03:55,550
meaning that as we
apply it repeatedly,

1335
01:03:55,550 --> 01:03:59,020
we went to this fixed point
in the tabular setting.

1336
01:03:59,020 --> 01:04:01,760
But the problem is that when
you do a Bellman backup,

1337
01:04:01,760 --> 01:04:03,472
that operator is a
contraction, meaning

1338
01:04:03,472 --> 01:04:05,930
that if you apply the Bellman
operator to different things,

1339
01:04:05,930 --> 01:04:08,290
their distance gets
smaller afterwards.

1340
01:04:08,290 --> 01:04:10,420
Value function
approximation fitting

1341
01:04:10,420 --> 01:04:13,180
can be an expansion, which
means if you take two things

1342
01:04:13,180 --> 01:04:16,040
and then you try to do value
function approximation,

1343
01:04:16,040 --> 01:04:18,560
like you align to this
one and align to this one,

1344
01:04:18,560 --> 01:04:20,470
the distance between
two points afterwards

1345
01:04:20,470 --> 01:04:22,810
can actually get bigger than
before you did the value

1346
01:04:22,810 --> 01:04:24,448
function approximation.

1347
01:04:24,448 --> 01:04:25,990
So there's a really
beautiful example

1348
01:04:25,990 --> 01:04:29,620
of this in a paper by
Jeff Gordon from 1995.

1349
01:04:29,620 --> 01:04:36,110
I will just-- Jeff Gordon
1995 has a really nice example

1350
01:04:36,110 --> 01:04:38,110
of this where you just
can kind of visually see,

1351
01:04:38,110 --> 01:04:40,610
when you have these two
functions and these points,

1352
01:04:40,610 --> 01:04:42,990
that after you do this value
function approximation,

1353
01:04:42,990 --> 01:04:46,227
you've actually made the
distance between them bigger.

1354
01:04:46,227 --> 01:04:48,560
And so that means that you
have this thing, where you're

1355
01:04:48,560 --> 01:04:50,727
kind of alternating between
something which you know

1356
01:04:50,727 --> 01:04:54,500
is a contraction and driving
you towards a fixed point,

1357
01:04:54,500 --> 01:04:57,740
and something which might
actually amplify differences.

1358
01:04:57,740 --> 01:04:59,840
And so because of that,
it's not always the case

1359
01:04:59,840 --> 01:05:02,720
that you're guaranteed to
converge to a fixed point.

1360
01:05:02,720 --> 01:05:04,540
So this is something
important to know.

1361
01:05:04,540 --> 01:05:07,700
However, I think
it's also kind of a--

1362
01:05:07,700 --> 01:05:10,535
it's an important part of the
history of the field in that,

1363
01:05:10,535 --> 01:05:13,850
in the 1990s, there was a bunch
of work showing that this could

1364
01:05:13,850 --> 01:05:17,030
occur that even with some really
simple settings like linear

1365
01:05:17,030 --> 01:05:19,880
value function approximators,
we just approximate things with

1366
01:05:19,880 --> 01:05:22,100
a line, and that sometimes
you could get these kind

1367
01:05:22,100 --> 01:05:24,270
of oscillations or
lack of convergence.

1368
01:05:24,270 --> 01:05:26,450
And so people were really
concerned about using

1369
01:05:26,450 --> 01:05:29,600
function approximators with
reinforcement learning.

1370
01:05:29,600 --> 01:05:33,170
But then what happened is that
DeepMind showed well, actually

1371
01:05:33,170 --> 01:05:34,880
there are some ways
to tackle this.

1372
01:05:34,880 --> 01:05:37,120
And we can do really
amazing things with it.

1373
01:05:37,120 --> 01:05:38,370
And so I think it's a useful--

1374
01:05:38,370 --> 01:05:40,250
like a useful
lesson from history

1375
01:05:40,250 --> 01:05:43,280
over the difference between,
well, what can occur

1376
01:05:43,280 --> 01:05:47,330
and maybe some sort of not
ideal cases versus what actually

1377
01:05:47,330 --> 01:05:48,600
occurs in practice.

1378
01:05:48,600 --> 01:05:52,220
And so we shouldn't let some
of the negative examples

1379
01:05:52,220 --> 01:05:54,110
limit us from
considering what might

1380
01:05:54,110 --> 01:05:56,270
work in some other scenarios.

1381
01:05:56,270 --> 01:05:57,360
So let's see that now.

1382
01:05:57,360 --> 01:06:00,170
Let's see DQN.

1383
01:06:00,170 --> 01:06:00,670
OK.

1384
01:06:00,670 --> 01:06:04,000
So the idea with
DQN is we're going

1385
01:06:04,000 --> 01:06:05,930
to use these ideas to
actually play Atari.

1386
01:06:05,930 --> 01:06:08,048
So we're going to take
in images of the game.

1387
01:06:08,048 --> 01:06:10,090
We're going to use
convolutional neural networks.

1388
01:06:10,090 --> 01:06:13,180
And we're going to have a
really big, deep neural network

1389
01:06:13,180 --> 01:06:17,200
to represent the Q-function
and do Q-learning with it.

1390
01:06:17,200 --> 01:06:21,700
So the idea was, well,
we knew that sometimes,

1391
01:06:21,700 --> 01:06:23,820
like Q-learning with value
function approximation

1392
01:06:23,820 --> 01:06:25,020
can diverge.

1393
01:06:25,020 --> 01:06:27,550
And there's a number
of different issues,

1394
01:06:27,550 --> 01:06:29,920
but one of them is kind
of this stability thing.

1395
01:06:29,920 --> 01:06:32,290
So we know that there's
correlations between samples.

1396
01:06:32,290 --> 01:06:34,740
Your data is not IID, which
is what you would normally

1397
01:06:34,740 --> 01:06:37,200
want for when you're doing
function approximation.

1398
01:06:37,200 --> 01:06:39,660
And the other is that you have
this kind of nonstationary

1399
01:06:39,660 --> 01:06:43,810
target thing, which is like when
you plug-in, say with learning,

1400
01:06:43,810 --> 01:06:45,600
you're plugging in gamma plus--

1401
01:06:45,600 --> 01:06:49,053
sorry, r plus gamma times
the value of your next state.

1402
01:06:49,053 --> 01:06:50,970
And that value of the
next state is constantly

1403
01:06:50,970 --> 01:06:53,640
changing as you get more data.

1404
01:06:53,640 --> 01:06:55,518
So what DQN did is
they said, well,

1405
01:06:55,518 --> 01:06:57,060
look, what we're
going to do is we're

1406
01:06:57,060 --> 01:06:58,920
going to use experience replay.

1407
01:06:58,920 --> 01:07:02,070
In particular, we're going
to reuse tuples over time.

1408
01:07:02,070 --> 01:07:04,415
And we're also going
to get fixed Q-targets.

1409
01:07:04,415 --> 01:07:05,790
And both of those
things ended up

1410
01:07:05,790 --> 01:07:09,323
making a really big difference,
particularly one of them.

1411
01:07:09,323 --> 01:07:10,240
We'll see in a second.

1412
01:07:10,240 --> 01:07:12,240
So the idea of
experience replay is

1413
01:07:12,240 --> 01:07:17,340
to say in general, if I think
about states that are nearby,

1414
01:07:17,340 --> 01:07:20,090
their Q-function might
be pretty similar.

1415
01:07:20,090 --> 01:07:21,650
And if I'm doing
lots of updates,

1416
01:07:21,650 --> 01:07:24,490
that's breaking my IID stuff
that I want for my function

1417
01:07:24,490 --> 01:07:25,798
approximation.

1418
01:07:25,798 --> 01:07:27,340
So another thing
you could do is just

1419
01:07:27,340 --> 01:07:30,490
have a replay buffer of lots of
all the different tuples you've

1420
01:07:30,490 --> 01:07:31,510
seen in the past.

1421
01:07:31,510 --> 01:07:33,940
And you could just
sample from one of those

1422
01:07:33,940 --> 01:07:36,610
and then compute a
target value and then

1423
01:07:36,610 --> 01:07:39,697
do stochastic gradient descent.

1424
01:07:39,697 --> 01:07:41,530
And this might be really
helpful anyway just

1425
01:07:41,530 --> 01:07:43,240
in terms of data
efficiency, because it

1426
01:07:43,240 --> 01:07:46,090
means that instead of taking
your data and using it once

1427
01:07:46,090 --> 01:07:48,220
and then throwing
it away, you keep it

1428
01:07:48,220 --> 01:07:49,940
and then you can replay it.

1429
01:07:49,940 --> 01:07:53,500
Just like how we talked about
batch learning last time.

1430
01:07:53,500 --> 01:07:55,390
So an experience
replay can be useful,

1431
01:07:55,390 --> 01:07:57,170
because we're both
replaying our data.

1432
01:07:57,170 --> 01:07:59,570
So we can squeeze more
information out of it.

1433
01:07:59,570 --> 01:08:01,750
And also we can select
from very different parts

1434
01:08:01,750 --> 01:08:05,870
of the past history, which makes
those updates more independent.

1435
01:08:05,870 --> 01:08:06,370
OK.

1436
01:08:06,370 --> 01:08:08,530
So this is-- and
in general, we're

1437
01:08:08,530 --> 01:08:10,910
not going to keep the
buffer for all time.

1438
01:08:10,910 --> 01:08:14,470
We might keep the last million
episodes or things like that.

1439
01:08:14,470 --> 01:08:18,140
OK, So that's one
thing we could do.

1440
01:08:18,140 --> 01:08:21,050
Now, the other
thing is that if we

1441
01:08:21,050 --> 01:08:23,899
think about what's happening
in this case, the way

1442
01:08:23,899 --> 01:08:28,279
we change the weights
is going to be--

1443
01:08:28,279 --> 01:08:32,939
in general, the weights
appear here and here and here.

1444
01:08:32,939 --> 01:08:36,705
So this target value is a
function of the weights itself,

1445
01:08:36,705 --> 01:08:38,330
because you're using
the value function

1446
01:08:38,330 --> 01:08:41,450
approximation to represent
the value of your next state.

1447
01:08:41,450 --> 01:08:43,410
And so the problem
is that in general,

1448
01:08:43,410 --> 01:08:45,439
this is going to change
on your next update,

1449
01:08:45,439 --> 01:08:48,950
because you've just
changed your weights.

1450
01:08:48,950 --> 01:08:51,020
And this can also
lead to instabilities,

1451
01:08:51,020 --> 01:08:54,649
because if you think of
supervised learning, your x, y

1452
01:08:54,649 --> 01:08:58,100
pairs, your y is changing
even for the same x over time,

1453
01:08:58,100 --> 01:09:01,370
because you're changing
your Q-function.

1454
01:09:01,370 --> 01:09:03,510
OK, because this is a
function of the weights.

1455
01:09:03,510 --> 01:09:06,229
And so as the weights
change, this target value

1456
01:09:06,229 --> 01:09:09,140
is going to change even
for the same input.

1457
01:09:09,140 --> 01:09:13,069
So the second idea is
to have fixed Q-updates.

1458
01:09:13,069 --> 01:09:15,210
And what the idea here
is-- and so remember,

1459
01:09:15,210 --> 01:09:17,279
this is like when we
say the target weights--

1460
01:09:17,279 --> 01:09:21,260
this is going to be what we're
using for the target weights--

1461
01:09:21,260 --> 01:09:22,810
is that the weight--

1462
01:09:22,810 --> 01:09:24,590
the weights or the
parameters we're

1463
01:09:24,590 --> 01:09:28,170
using to estimate the value
of the next state we reach,

1464
01:09:28,170 --> 01:09:31,520
we are going to not
update those as much.

1465
01:09:31,520 --> 01:09:33,228
So we're going to have
our target network

1466
01:09:33,228 --> 01:09:35,228
using a different set of
weights and the weights

1467
01:09:35,228 --> 01:09:36,540
that are being updated.

1468
01:09:36,540 --> 01:09:39,300
So you can see here
that we have a w minus,

1469
01:09:39,300 --> 01:09:41,401
meaning that we're
trying to make this more

1470
01:09:41,401 --> 01:09:43,609
like supervised learning
where we have a fixed output

1471
01:09:43,609 --> 01:09:48,740
y that is not changing, while
we're trying to update our w.

1472
01:09:48,740 --> 01:09:51,120
And so if you think
about the example,

1473
01:09:51,120 --> 01:09:53,100
we want to just
draw it like this.

1474
01:09:53,100 --> 01:09:56,540
Here's our states,
here's our Q-function.

1475
01:09:56,540 --> 01:09:58,580
Right now, we'd
like to make sure

1476
01:09:58,580 --> 01:10:00,290
that these points,
when we're trying

1477
01:10:00,290 --> 01:10:03,240
to fit a line that those y's
are not changing a lot where

1478
01:10:03,240 --> 01:10:04,740
we're trying to fit the line.

1479
01:10:04,740 --> 01:10:06,570
And in general, because
they're a function

1480
01:10:06,570 --> 01:10:09,730
of the weights themselves, they
might be moving and perturbing.

1481
01:10:09,730 --> 01:10:12,580
And so what we're saying is,
no, we're going to fix these.

1482
01:10:12,580 --> 01:10:15,600
So you can think of this as just
being a fixed number for a while

1483
01:10:15,600 --> 01:10:18,203
and then do multiple
updates on this w

1484
01:10:18,203 --> 01:10:19,370
to try to fit that function.

1485
01:10:22,450 --> 01:10:26,530
And so what that means is we
just have to we keep around

1486
01:10:26,530 --> 01:10:31,150
these target weights, and we
keep around the other weights.

1487
01:10:31,150 --> 01:10:33,100
And this allows us
to do-- this is what

1488
01:10:33,100 --> 01:10:35,620
is called the fixed Q-updating.

1489
01:10:35,620 --> 01:10:37,840
So if you think about
what this pseudocode would

1490
01:10:37,840 --> 01:10:41,540
look like in this case--

1491
01:10:41,540 --> 01:10:48,583
let's see-- is
it's going to look

1492
01:10:48,583 --> 01:10:50,250
pretty similar to the
things we've seen.

1493
01:10:50,250 --> 01:10:51,540
You're going to
sample an action.

1494
01:10:51,540 --> 01:10:52,860
You're going to observe
a word in the next state.

1495
01:10:52,860 --> 01:10:55,350
You're going to store the
transition in a replay buffer.

1496
01:10:55,350 --> 01:10:56,850
So you're going to
keep track of it.

1497
01:10:56,850 --> 01:10:59,225
Then you're going to sample
a random mini batch of tuples

1498
01:10:59,225 --> 01:11:00,783
from the past.

1499
01:11:00,783 --> 01:11:02,450
You're going to do
something, keep track

1500
01:11:02,450 --> 01:11:03,900
of the episodes terminated.

1501
01:11:03,900 --> 01:11:05,970
Otherwise, you're
going to say my target

1502
01:11:05,970 --> 01:11:08,720
y that I'm going to try to fit
in my function approximator

1503
01:11:08,720 --> 01:11:11,120
is my immediate reward,
plus the maximum

1504
01:11:11,120 --> 01:11:15,313
of my actions of my Q-function
with my target weights.

1505
01:11:15,313 --> 01:11:17,480
So I use my deep neural
network to predict the value

1506
01:11:17,480 --> 01:11:19,100
of that state action pair.

1507
01:11:19,100 --> 01:11:20,930
And then I'm going to
do gradient descent

1508
01:11:20,930 --> 01:11:23,780
on the difference between
these predicted y's

1509
01:11:23,780 --> 01:11:26,600
and my current estimate
with my current weights.

1510
01:11:26,600 --> 01:11:29,270
So this is just the
function fitting part.

1511
01:11:29,270 --> 01:11:30,950
And then you repeat this.

1512
01:11:30,950 --> 01:11:34,950
And then periodically, you
update your target weights.

1513
01:11:34,950 --> 01:11:35,770
OK?

1514
01:11:35,770 --> 01:11:37,310
And I just want
to highlight here,

1515
01:11:37,310 --> 01:11:39,370
there's a bunch of different
choices to be made.

1516
01:11:39,370 --> 01:11:41,620
You have to decide, what
function approximator are you

1517
01:11:41,620 --> 01:11:42,120
using?

1518
01:11:42,120 --> 01:11:44,020
Are you using a
deep neural network?

1519
01:11:44,020 --> 01:11:45,320
What's your learning rate?

1520
01:11:45,320 --> 01:11:47,620
How often to update
the target weight?

1521
01:11:47,620 --> 01:11:49,430
How big should your
replay buffer be?

1522
01:11:49,430 --> 01:11:51,805
There's a lot of different
choices that you have to make.

1523
01:11:55,070 --> 01:11:55,570
All right.

1524
01:11:55,570 --> 01:11:59,290
Let's just take a quick second
here to see if this part's--

1525
01:11:59,290 --> 01:12:02,730
like a chance to
think about this part.

1526
01:12:02,730 --> 01:12:04,950
You may have just seen
the answer, but that's OK.

1527
01:12:04,950 --> 01:12:07,057
Which is-- OK, in
DQN, we're going

1528
01:12:07,057 --> 01:12:09,390
to compute the target value
for the sampled state action

1529
01:12:09,390 --> 01:12:12,870
reward next states using a
separate set of target weights.

1530
01:12:12,870 --> 01:12:15,580
So does that change
the computation time?

1531
01:12:15,580 --> 01:12:18,380
Does it change the
memory requirements?

1532
01:12:18,380 --> 01:12:21,400
Are you not sure?

1533
01:12:21,400 --> 01:12:22,370
Put that in here.

1534
01:12:26,800 --> 01:12:29,380
We're now going to maintain
two different sets of weights

1535
01:12:29,380 --> 01:12:31,160
to do our function
approximation.

1536
01:13:30,100 --> 01:13:31,840
All right.

1537
01:13:31,840 --> 01:13:32,350
Yep.

1538
01:13:32,350 --> 01:13:35,210
I see almost everyone
converged to the right answer

1539
01:13:35,210 --> 01:13:35,810
very quickly.

1540
01:13:35,810 --> 01:13:37,550
It is doubling the
memory requirements.

1541
01:13:37,550 --> 01:13:41,860
So you have to keep track of
a second set of parameters.

1542
01:13:41,860 --> 01:13:43,940
It does not change
the computation time.

1543
01:13:43,940 --> 01:13:46,450
It just changes the
memory requirements.

1544
01:13:46,450 --> 01:13:47,825
So we just keep
around two copies

1545
01:13:47,825 --> 01:13:50,075
of your deep neural network,
one with the old weights,

1546
01:13:50,075 --> 01:13:51,110
one with the new ones.

1547
01:13:51,110 --> 01:13:55,130
And then the Q-updating with
respect to that is the same.

1548
01:13:55,130 --> 01:13:57,270
All right, let's see
what that actually does.

1549
01:13:57,270 --> 01:13:59,690
So the kind of the key
innovations for DQN

1550
01:13:59,690 --> 01:14:02,270
where we are going to use deep
neural networks that had been

1551
01:14:02,270 --> 01:14:03,917
done before, but not with--

1552
01:14:03,917 --> 01:14:05,750
I think this is the
first really big example

1553
01:14:05,750 --> 01:14:08,750
with convolutional
neural networks.

1554
01:14:08,750 --> 01:14:12,620
It's going to maintain these
really large episodic replays.

1555
01:14:12,620 --> 01:14:16,890
And then it is also going
to have these fixed targets.

1556
01:14:16,890 --> 01:14:17,410
All right.

1557
01:14:17,410 --> 01:14:19,920
So what they have
here is they're

1558
01:14:19,920 --> 01:14:22,170
going to do these series
of convolutions, output.

1559
01:14:22,170 --> 01:14:24,760
And they're going to output
a Q value for each action.

1560
01:14:24,760 --> 01:14:27,510
And they're going to use
that to make decisions.

1561
01:14:27,510 --> 01:14:29,222
And I think one of
the things-- well,

1562
01:14:29,222 --> 01:14:30,930
there's multiple really
remarkable things

1563
01:14:30,930 --> 01:14:32,010
about this paper.

1564
01:14:32,010 --> 01:14:34,290
One is that they got
extremely good performance

1565
01:14:34,290 --> 01:14:36,320
across a really
wide set of games.

1566
01:14:36,320 --> 01:14:38,320
So instead of only having
a few benchmark tasks,

1567
01:14:38,320 --> 01:14:40,320
they looked at the whole
suite of performance.

1568
01:14:40,320 --> 01:14:43,690
They are learning a different
policy per video game,

1569
01:14:43,690 --> 01:14:45,750
but it is the same neural
network architecture,

1570
01:14:45,750 --> 01:14:48,150
and I believe, all the
same hyperparameters too.

1571
01:14:48,150 --> 01:14:49,990
So the idea with
that is to say like,

1572
01:14:49,990 --> 01:14:51,570
could we actually
have the same type

1573
01:14:51,570 --> 01:14:54,000
of architecture in the same
way that we don't swap brains

1574
01:14:54,000 --> 01:14:57,900
when we do different tasks,
but have the same learning

1575
01:14:57,900 --> 01:14:59,640
algorithm, learn
to be able to do

1576
01:14:59,640 --> 01:15:01,055
many different types of tasks?

1577
01:15:01,055 --> 01:15:03,180
And so I think that was
pretty impressive that they

1578
01:15:03,180 --> 01:15:04,430
showed that that was possible.

1579
01:15:04,430 --> 01:15:06,790
So you have the same algorithm,
same hyperparameters,

1580
01:15:06,790 --> 01:15:10,812
but it could learn to do
well in many different tasks.

1581
01:15:10,812 --> 01:15:13,020
I think one of the interesting
things about the paper

1582
01:15:13,020 --> 01:15:15,240
is to consider what
were the aspects that

1583
01:15:15,240 --> 01:15:16,990
were important for success.

1584
01:15:16,990 --> 01:15:19,980
So here's just a
subset of algorithms--

1585
01:15:19,980 --> 01:15:21,400
or sorry, a subset
of the domains.

1586
01:15:21,400 --> 01:15:23,500
This is a few of the games.

1587
01:15:23,500 --> 01:15:26,980
And they also compared to using
a much more simple function

1588
01:15:26,980 --> 01:15:28,050
approximator.

1589
01:15:28,050 --> 01:15:31,780
And what you can see here is
that the deep neural network

1590
01:15:31,780 --> 01:15:33,030
is not actually better, right?

1591
01:15:33,030 --> 01:15:35,550
Like the deep neural
network does not look better

1592
01:15:35,550 --> 01:15:38,980
than the linear case.

1593
01:15:38,980 --> 01:15:40,620
So it's not clear
that just using

1594
01:15:40,620 --> 01:15:43,350
a more function approximate--
like it wasn't just

1595
01:15:43,350 --> 01:15:48,520
that they used a much more
careful function approximator.

1596
01:15:48,520 --> 01:15:51,920
And the second thing was
whether they use this fixed Q,

1597
01:15:51,920 --> 01:15:53,740
and that helped.

1598
01:15:53,740 --> 01:15:55,960
So you can see now
that they are exceeding

1599
01:15:55,960 --> 01:15:59,060
the performance of using a more
simple function approximator.

1600
01:15:59,060 --> 01:16:01,600
So this idea of
keeping things stable

1601
01:16:01,600 --> 01:16:05,020
is helpful in terms
of oscillations.

1602
01:16:05,020 --> 01:16:09,530
But using the replay
was incredibly helpful.

1603
01:16:09,530 --> 01:16:15,700
So they went from 3 or 10
up to 241, or in some--

1604
01:16:15,700 --> 01:16:17,620
something from either
roughly three times

1605
01:16:17,620 --> 01:16:20,110
as good and sometimes even
more like a couple orders

1606
01:16:20,110 --> 01:16:21,520
of magnitude.

1607
01:16:21,520 --> 01:16:25,880
So it was incredibly helpful to
use an experience replay buffer.

1608
01:16:25,880 --> 01:16:27,760
And maybe this
isn't so surprising,

1609
01:16:27,760 --> 01:16:31,870
because it means that they are
just reusing their data lot.

1610
01:16:31,870 --> 01:16:34,395
But it was really,
incredibly important.

1611
01:16:34,395 --> 01:16:35,770
And I think that's
really helpful

1612
01:16:35,770 --> 01:16:37,870
to motivate why thinking
about sample efficiency

1613
01:16:37,870 --> 01:16:41,030
and reusing your
data is helpful.

1614
01:16:41,030 --> 01:16:43,760
And then combining these ideas
led to even bigger benefits.

1615
01:16:43,760 --> 01:16:47,200
So it was helpful to have both
the fixed targets and the replay

1616
01:16:47,200 --> 01:16:47,720
buffer.

1617
01:16:47,720 --> 01:16:49,762
But if you could only pick
one, the replay buffer

1618
01:16:49,762 --> 01:16:53,310
was just enormously helpful.

1619
01:16:53,310 --> 01:16:54,040
All right.

1620
01:16:54,040 --> 01:16:57,168
So as you guys know, there's
been an enormous amount

1621
01:16:57,168 --> 01:16:58,710
of interest in
reinforcement learning

1622
01:16:58,710 --> 01:17:00,660
and deep reinforcement
learning since.

1623
01:17:00,660 --> 01:17:02,580
There was some immediate
improvements kind

1624
01:17:02,580 --> 01:17:04,470
of within the next year or two.

